\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Switzerland surprises with its first rate hike since 2007 and UK rate hike hits 2009 highs\b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has decided to tighten its monetary policy stance with a 50 basis point hike in the benchmark rate and overnight deposit interest rate, the first increase since September 2007, to -0.25% to counter rising inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision comes hours after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raised rates by 75 basis points and comes ahead of the 25 basis point hike announced by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ) for July.\par \par \uc2 "Tighter monetary policy aims to prevent inflation from spreading more broadly to goods and services in Switzerland," the bank explained, warning that "further increases in the future cannot be ruled out to stabilize inflation in a range consistent with price stability over the medium term."\par \par \uc2 Fed implements most aggressive rate hike in nearly three decades and cuts U.S. growth forecast.All economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  prior to the decision were expecting no changes to be announced at this morning's meeting.\par \par \uc2 In addition to tightening its monetary policy, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has stated that, in order to ensure appropriate monetary conditions, it is also prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation rate reached 2.9% in May in May and is likely to remain at an elevated level for some time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated, which anticipates a price rise of 2.8% by 2022, 1.9% by 2023 and 1.6% by 2024, noting that "without today's rate hike, the inflation forecast would be significantly higher."\par \par \uc2 It also notes that global economic growth has slowed markedly as a result of high inflation, which is weighing on purchasing power and thus reducing demand, as well as uncertainty stemming from the war in Ukraine, while the coronavirus confinements in China are holding back the global market.\par \par \uc2 Thus, for 2022, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  anticipates GDP growth of around 2.5% under the assumption that the global economy continues to grow and that the intensity of the war in Ukraine does not increase.\par \par \uc2 London adds to the upsideThe \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  (BOE) raised interest rates by a quarter percentage point on Thursday despite pressure for a bolder move to combat price increases that have pushed inflation to its highest level in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Although it began raising interest rates earlier than its counterparts, the U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is lagging behind the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  in the global fight against inflation fueled by high food and energy prices.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. benchmark rate is at 1.25% after rising 25 basis points for the fifth consecutive hike.\par \par \uc2 The Fed raised its benchmark rate by three-quarters of a percentage point on Wednesday to a range between 1.5% and 1.75%.\par \par \uc2 The war in Ukraine has raised food and energy prices by affecting shipments of oil, natural gas, grains and cooking oil. This is on top of price increases that began last year as the economy began to recover after the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  forecast last month that inflation would rise to more than 10% in the coming months, after reaching 9% in April, the highest since 1982. The bank's target is 2% inflation.\par \par \uc2 The bank has chosen not to raise interest rates too quickly because it argues that many of the inflationary pressures on the British economy are external and beyond its control.\par \par \uc2 But price increases, as they become entrenched in the economy, drive calls for wage increases and dampen growth in the economy by reducing consumer and business purchases.\par \par \uc2 The BOE "continues to balance the inflationary effects on the economy, with the real possibility that they will tighten too much and the U.K. economy will fall into a full-blown recession," said Alan Custis, managing director at \uc2 Lazard Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hungary also unexpectedly raised rates amid the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attempt to prop up the forint, which fell to 400 per euro for the first time this week.\par \par \uc2 Continued in Pulse Job creation manages to offset fall in real wages and pushes up labor incomeFuel prices: How much will gasoline go up this Thursday?Spotify to reduce hiring due to economic uncertaintyU.S. court approves financial reorganization of Corp Group BankingIkea puts Russian factories up for sale, plans job cuts\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220616ei6g007d9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
