\par\par \b \uc2 EUR/CHF's Decline Spurs Possibility of SNB  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0757 GMT - EUR/CHF's decline to a fresh seven-year low in the Asian session spurs the risk of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. There will certainly be an effort to stop Swiss franc appreciation near the 1.0000 level against the euro, but if price stability is \uc2 SNB\uc2 's top priority, policymakers may not get too aggressive on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  says. EUR/CHF edges 0.1% lower to 1.0014 after earlier touching 0.9974, the lowest intraday level since January 2015, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT - Bond yields are expected to drop moderately in the next three to six months, LBBW's analysts say. For the Federal Reserve, LBBW's analysts still expect five to six interest rate rises in 2022, but the likelihood of "super-sized" interest rate increases of 50 basis points has probably faded for now, they add. They cut their 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield forecast for the year-end to 2.25% from 2.50%, keeping the mid-2023 yield forecast at 2.70%. They expect the 10-year Bund yield at 0.30% at end-2022, down from 0.50% expected previously, while the mid-2023 forecast remains at 0.70%. The 10-year Bund yield trades at -0.056%, up 3.2 bps, while the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield trades at 1.733%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT - For the eurozone economy, the war in Ukraine and the firm Western response are a huge external shock, mainly coming from a big one-off spike in import costs, Berenberg says. Eurozone inflation looks set to surge to around 7% soon--or even more--Berenberg forecasts. But inflation will almost certainly recede significantly thereafter, Berenberg says. This is because unlike the U.S., the eurozone doesn't exhibit any home-grown inflationary dynamic. "Near term, the eurozone will likely remain in the near-stagflation into which it had fallen with the Delta wave of Covid-19 infections in November 202--as the impact of the war offsets the post-Omicron rebound in services," Berenberg says. A recession, however, looks unlikely, Berenberg adds. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0720 GMT - Safe-haven assets are unlikely to lose their attractiveness in the short term because of the war in Ukraine, \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank\uc2  's analysts say. "As we do not expect an immediate easing of the conflict, but even a further intensification could still be ahead, we do not think that safe havens should lose their attractiveness in the short term," they say. The German Bund curve reflects intensified demand for safe havens, and an adjustment, but not a reversal, of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's interest rate cycle, they say. \uc2 Raiffeisen\uc2 's analysts expect an incipient monetary tightening by the \uc2 ECB\uc2  in 2022 to be likely, but the war in Ukraine represents a major uncertainty factor for the extent and timing of the monetary policy outlook. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT - South Korea's benchmark Kospi fell 2.3% to close at 2651.31 on receding risk appetite amid the deepening Russia-Ukraine conflict. Tech and transport stocks led the declines. The intensifying war in Ukraine made investors more pessimistic about the prospect for any near-term relief to the disruption in global supply chains and inflation. U.S. lawmakers' call to ban Russian oil purchases to punish Russia for its invasion of Ukraine risks higher prices further at the pump, which could hurt corporate profits. USD/KRW rose 1.1% to a 21-month high of 1,227.10 on the risk aversion. Index heavyweight \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  fell 2.0%. Memory-chip maker \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  lost 4.0%. Shipping firm \uc2 HMM\uc2  slumped 4.5%. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0656 GMT - EUR/USD may fall below 1.0805 on the technical charts following its recent slide, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says, noting 1.0805 represents support on a long-term rising trendline connecting the lows of 2017 and 2020. Last week, EUR/USD dropped below major support at 1.1085 and extended losses to break below 1.1000 and 1.0900 to post its biggest 1-week drop in two years, \uc2 UOB\uc2  says. Given EUR/USD's impulsive downward momentum, a break below 1.0805 wouldn't be surprising, \uc2 UOB\uc2  adds. EUR/USD is down 0.5% at 1.0877 after earlier touching 1.0824, the lowest intraday level since May 2020, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT - China's export volumes will likely plateau in the coming months, as ports are already stretched to capacity and the war in Ukraine threatens to disrupt supply chains further, says Julian Evans-Pritchard, senior China economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . Surging prices will also damp demand for Chinese goods, he says. China's exports growth decelerated to 16.3% in January and February from 20.9% in December, official data show. Evans-Pritchard attributes the slowdown to a high base effect last year and reckons the main driver this year was a sharp pickup in exports of furniture and recreational products. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT - USD/SGD rises as increasing worry over the Russia-Ukraine war spurs safe-haven demand for the greenback. Market sentiment is fragile amid a growing consensus that the Ukraine crisis will have a discernible effect on global supply chains and economic activity, Maybank says. On daily technical charts, USD/SGD's momentum is bullish, although the relative strength index is approaching overbought territory, Maybank says, pegging initial resistance at 1.3670 and initial support at 1.3580. USD/SGD is up 0.2% at 1.3634 after earlier touching 1.3644, the highest intraday level since Dec. 22, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0622 GMT - Japanese stocks closed lower, dragged by falls in tech and auto stocks, as concerns grew about the war in Ukraine and its impact on global trade. \uc2 Lasertec\uc2  fell 9.3% and \uc2 Isuzu Motors\uc2  dropped 9.1%. Meanwhile, energy company \uc2 Idemitsu Kosan\uc2  gained 6.8% and \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  rose 4.3%. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 2.9% to 25221.41. Headlines on Ukraine would remain in focus after the U.S. said it was in discussions with allies on banning Russian oil imports. USD/JPY was at 114.93, compared with 114.82 late Friday in New York. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell one basis point to 0.140%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0527 GMT - \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still raise interest rates this year despite the uncertainty surrounding the global economy, particularly in commodities prices and financial markets, says \uc2 Fitch Solutions\uc2  . With the Fed widely anticipated to tighten monetary policy this year, \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  may follow suit to maintain its real interest rate differential with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it says. The Malaysian economy should continue recovering in 2022, giving \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  sufficient room to deploy monetary policy normalization to tame inflation, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  says. It expects \uc2 Bank Negara\uc2  to raise its overnight policy rate by 50 basis points to 2.25% this year. (chester.tay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT - Prices for Australian luxury cars and top-of-the-market houses are starting to slip, which CommSec says could be a leading indicator for other parts of the market. A shift from car and home purchases to overseas travel may be one of the trends that emerges over the next few months, it adds. The CommSec index of luxury new vehicle sales fell to a 16-month low of 82,445 vehicles in the year to February. Annual sales are down 2.7%, the biggest fall in 12 months. Meanwhile, \uc2 CoreLogic\uc2  says home prices for the top 25% of homes by value rose 0.2% in February, the slowest growth in 16 months. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT - Consumer price pressures in Australia have broadened, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . Prices of more items are now rising at a pace above 1% on-quarter than at any point since the global financial crisis, it adds. While petrol and the cost of new homes accounted for 40% of the 1.3% on-quarter rise in consumer prices in 4Q, business surveys suggest that cost pressures have intensified sharply in recent months. And with trimmed mean inflation set to approach 4% on-year soon, expect the \uc2 RBA\uc2  to start raising interest rates in June, it says. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 07, 2022 03:00 ET (08:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220307ei37000mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
