\par\par \b \uc2 SNB\uc2  Likely to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  if EUR/CHF Parity Rapidly Broken -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 10:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0942 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain the rise of the safe-haven Swiss franc due to Russia's continuing invasion of Ukraine if the EUR/CHF rate drops "rapidly" below parity, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  analysts say in a research note. EUR/CHF briefly broke below parity for the first time since 2015 in overnight trade, reaching a low of 0.9973, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , before recovering to last trade at 1.0048. "We expect the \uc2 SNB\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the parity threshold is rapidly broken," the analysts say. However, given that inflation is now accelerating in Switzerland, the \uc2 SNB\uc2  should be under less pressure to counteract the sharp appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it was in 2015, they say. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no power to control inflation which is driven by external supply shocks, a \uc2 Swedbank\uc2  analyst says, adding that the \uc2 ECB\uc2  shouldn't try fighting this inflation with tighter monetary policy. Higher interest rates wouldn't help companies and households that are facing record high energy and food prices, thus, "what Europe needs now is low interest rates and spreads, so that it can invest even more in sustainable energy infrastructure and defence," the analyst says. Even if the \uc2 ECB\uc2  decides it wants to exit the negative interest rate environment, asset purchases should and are likely to continue, the analyst says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT - Euro corporate bonds which are eligible for purchase by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to outperform as investors brace for the impact of a possible Western ban on oil imports from Russia, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's research team. "Similar to what we have observed since the outbreak of the Russia-Ukraine crisis - investment grade paper, notably Corporate Sector Purchase Program-eligible bonds are likely to outperform," analysts at the Italian bank say. News of the possible ban has rattled markets, with investors dumping stocks and buying perceived safer bonds. The pan-continental Stoxx Europe 600 falls 3.7%, while European government bond prices gain.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT - German Bund yields are unlikely to stay below 0% for very long unless the economic hit from the war in Ukraine is enough to remove the need for monetary policy normalization altogether, say ING's rates strategists. However, a removal of the need to normalize monetary policy isn't ING strategists' base case. "We suspect this won't be the case, but it will take time to answer that question," they say. At the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's meeting this week, the strategists expect the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to play it safe, adding that the rise in systemic risk indicators shows that the impact of escalating geopolitical tensions isn't yet known. The 10-year Bund yield is trading 1 basis point higher at -0.078%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT - Orders received by German manufacturing increased 1.8% in January compared with the previous month, showing that demand for industrial goods remains strong, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. However, the determining factor for industrial production remains the quantity of intermediate products available, the German bank says. There are signs that the availability of input products has improved, at least into February, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . However, the war in Ukraine has now added another uncertainty factor, it notes. This is because the automotive industry, among others, obtains many intermediate products from this country, so that the loss of deliveries would once again slow down industrial production, the bank says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0828 GMT - Manufacturing orders rose 1.8% on month in January, propelled primarily by foreign demand, and year-on-year growth in new orders seems to be picking up, as the level of demand remained robust at the start of the year, Pantheon Macroeconomics says. "This doesn't mean much if the supply side is holding back production, but here too, the numbers are improving," economists at Pantheon say. Industrial turnover--usually a good leading indicator for month-to-month changes in production--rose by 1.8%, after an upwardly-revised 0.7% increase in December, pointing to a robust expansion in output, Pantheon says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT - A surge in demand for dollars due to Russia's continued invasion of Ukraine and the eurozone economy's vulnerability due to geographical proximity to the war could cause the euro to slide further toward $1.05, ING analysts say in a note. "EUR/USD has decisively broken below 1.10 as this most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair is used as the proxy hedge for European \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general," they say. EUR/USD dropped to a 22-month low of $1.0824 overnight, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , and could now target chart support at 1.0760/70 area and then the March 2020 lows of 1.0640, the analysts say. However, they note that at times of stress like this, "big figures of e.g. 1.0500 become more relevant." EUR/USD is last down 0.5% at 1.0875. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT - The dollar jumps as demand for the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surges due to Russia's continued invasion of Ukraine and the possibility that sanctions could include a potential ban on imports of Russian oil. The DXY dollar index surges to 99.2130 overnight, its strongest since May 2020. "Expect the dollar to remain in demand today and DXY to continue heading towards 100," ING analysts say in a note. Dollar demand and concerns about the impact of the Ukraine war on the European economy cause the euro to dive to a 22-month low of $1.0824 overnight. The DXY is last at 98.8940 and EUR/USD at 1.0877. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT - The prospect of higher defense and energy infrastructure expenditures might help with growth over the medium to long term, and it suggests that risks are broadly skewed to the upside on funding and issuance in the eurozone government bond market, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  's rates strategists say. Risks are biased higher for EUR rates, they say. This is especially the case given the market has already priced out a significant amount of interest rates rises from the EUR curve, they add. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT - EUR/CHF's decline to a fresh seven-year low in the Asian session spurs the risk of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. There will certainly be an effort to stop Swiss franc appreciation near the 1.0000 level against the euro, but if price stability is \uc2 SNB\uc2 's top priority, policymakers may not get too aggressive on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, \uc2 Swissquote\uc2  says. EUR/CHF edges 0.1% lower to 1.0014 after earlier touching 0.9974, the lowest intraday level since January 2015, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT - Bond yields are expected to drop moderately in the next three to six months, LBBW's analysts say. For the Federal Reserve, LBBW's analysts still expect five to six interest rate rises in 2022, but the likelihood of "super-sized" interest rate increases of 50 basis points has probably faded for now, they add. They cut their 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield forecast for the year-end to 2.25% from 2.50%, keeping the mid-2023 yield forecast at 2.70%. They expect the 10-year Bund yield at 0.30% at end-2022, down from 0.50% expected previously, while the mid-2023 forecast remains at 0.70%. The 10-year Bund yield trades at -0.056%, up 3.2 bps, while the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield trades at 1.733%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT - For the eurozone economy, the war in Ukraine and the firm Western response are a huge external shock, mainly coming from a big one-off spike in import costs, Berenberg says. Eurozone inflation looks set to surge to around 7% soon--or even more--Berenberg forecasts. But inflation will almost certainly recede significantly thereafter, Berenberg says. This is because unlike the U.S., the eurozone doesn't exhibit any home-grown inflationary dynamic. "Near term, the eurozone will likely remain in the near-stagflation into which it had fallen with the Delta wave of Covid-19 infections in November 202--as the impact of the war offsets the post-Omicron rebound in services," Berenberg says. A recession, however, looks unlikely, Berenberg adds. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 07, 2022 04:42 ET (09:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220307ei37000rg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
