\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland \highlight\uc2  steps up  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  vs flying franc\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2021\par 15:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds SNB response)\par \par \uc2 By John Revill\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Dec 13 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's sight deposits rose by their highest amount in seven months last week, indicating policymakers are taking the first decisive steps to battle a rise in the value of the franc against its major rivals, particularly the euro.\par \par \uc2 Total sight deposits, which include other deposits on sight in Swiss francs, rose to 722.718 billion francs from 720.336 billion francs in the previous week, according to data from the SNB.\par \par \uc2 The 2.4 billion franc increase last week was the largest rise since May 20, indicating a change of tactics by the SNB which had appeared to be sidelined even as the franc hit its highest value versus the euro since July 2015 on Dec. 3.\par \par \uc2 A rise can indicate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the franc, a key plank of the SNB's strategy over the past six years.\par \par \uc2 The SNB buys foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from commercial banks in exchange for newly-created francs which are then credited to their sight deposit accounts held by the SNB.\par \par \uc2 Previous weeks have seen only small increases in sight deposits as the franc broke through the 1.05 barrier versus the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued to strengthen. It has gained more than 5% since mid-September versus the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The SNB, which is due to announce its latest monetary policy decision on Thursday, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB appears to have stepped up its fight against CHF strength", said \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in a note, adding Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  was probably concerned more about the speed of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation rather than the level it has reached.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in EUR-CHF over the last 12 weeks has been the fastest since the de-peg," the bank added referring to the euro/swiss franc exchange rate by its popular moniker.\par \par \uc2 UBS economist Alessandro Bee said the increase in sight deposits could mean the SNB was not willing to let the EUR-CHF exchange rate slip below 1.04 in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Karsten Junius, an economist at \uc2 J.Safra Sarasin\uc2 , said the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases were likely to have been higher than the 2.4 billion franc rise in sight deposits because the seasonal cash withdrawal during the run-up to Christmas means sight deposits usually fall at this time of year.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB did not feel comfortable with the speed of the exchange rate appreciation that we have seen lately. Especially, the move below 1,04 EUR-CHF and the closer vicinity of the 1,03 level that we consider to be crucial might have triggered stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Junius said.\par \par \uc2 He said he expected even stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the SNB in the coming weeks in case the franc moved towards 1.03 versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by John Revill; Editing by Saikat Chatterjee and Ed Osmond) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-12-13T15:50:44.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020211213ehcd02iqp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
