\par\par \b \uc2 VP Bank AG\uc2  - Dollar and franc flying high \u8230\'20\'26 for now\b0\par\par\uc2 VP Bank AG published this content on 19 Nov 2021 and is solely responsible for the information contained herein. Distributed by PUBT, unedited and unaltered, on 19 Nov 2021 13:25:24 UTC. \par \uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2021\par \uc2 Public Companies News and Documents via PUBT\par \uc2 LCDVP\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021. As included in the Information \par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.publicnow.com/1FC80BA6DC3E0E5D2FE3462F92CA724ABF6DA1CB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Original Document}}}\par \par \uc2 Dollar and franc flying high \u8230\'20\'26 for now\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, the financial markets believe that, even in the short term, the ECB and SNB will act in tandem rather than the Swiss monetary authorities waiting for Frankfurt to make the first move.\par \par \uc2 In response to the franc's strength, the SNB has recommenced its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It appears that any move below the EUR/CHF 1.05 mark will be hindered at least for the time being. However, as the recent market movement shows, even 1.05 is not considered a strict lower limit for the SNB. Since the summer months, commercial banks' sight deposits with the SNB have increased by roughly CHF 8 billion. This can be viewed as an indicator of the extent to which the SNB has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Structural strengths of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 There are probably two reasons why players believe that the SNB has greater scope for rate hikes in the longer run:\par \par \uc2 On one hand, both the benchmark rate and the deposit rate for commercial banks in Switzerland stand at -0.75 %, compared to the ECB's main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MRO) rate of 0% and the deposit rate of -0.5%. The SNB therefore has farther to go on the road to normalising monetary policy than does the ECB and consequently will need to raise rates more significantly before they are back in positive territory.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, structural aspects could also be behind the market's expectations of the SNB in terms of rate hikes. The ECB holds the monetary reins over a heterogeneous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  realm. The differing financial and economic policies of the member states require that the weakest one be taken into consideration in any move on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Precisely for this reason, eurozone interest rates could be lower than those in Switzerland at some point in the not-too-distant future. The Swiss Confederation is one of the most competitive economies in the world, a factor which over the next several years could lead to higher growth rates and thus higher interest rates than in the eurozone. Since the ECB at present is increasingly and repeatedly speaking of uniform financing conditions within the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area, this may indeed be an indication of Frankfurt's intention to act in favour of the weaker and not the strongest member states. By doing so, however, there is a risk that even in the event of persistently high inflation, the ECB will not respond adequately with a stricter interest rate policy. It is precisely this concern that could explain the franc's current strength against the euro, something that would also argue for the continued buoyancy of the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the longer run. Purchasing power parity (PPP) supports this notion as well (see chart).\par \par \uc2 EUR/CHF and purchasing power parity\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vpbank.com/en/news/2021/dollar-and-franc-flying-high-now"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Original Link}}}\par \par \uc2 Disclaimer\par \par \uc2 VP Bank AG\uc2  published this content on 19 November 2021 and is solely responsible for the information contained therein. Distributed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.publicnow.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public}}}\uc2 , unedited and unaltered, on 19 November 2021 13:27:13 UTC.\par \par \uc2 Dokument LCDVP00020211119ehbj008kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
