vi\u241\'00\'F1o participates in G20 online meeting with Finance ministers in Madrid \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 G20 Finance Ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governors hold news conference during the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 /IMF annual meetings in Washington, D.C \u8226\'20\'22 18:00 Federal Open Market Committee will release the minutes from its September 21 - 22, 2021 policy meeting \u8226\'20\'22 20:30 Kansas Fed's George gives introductory remarks and Fed's \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2  speaks on "Engaging Tribal Leaders on Financial Inclusion and the Economic Challenges of the Pandemic" before hybrid virtual "Fed Listens: Roundtable with Oklahoma Tribal Leaders", in Washington, D.C Commentary and Analysis Bearish EUR/USD bets take their toll on the downtrend \u8226\'20\'22 By Oct 5 EUR/USD traders were net short for first time since March 2020 \u8226\'20\'22 Although net short small, shorts have grown hugely, little room to rise more \u8226\'20\'22 Shorts have grown seven-fold in last 58 weeks \u8226\'20\'22 Shorts now 219k contracts, record bet is 271k contracts \u8226\'20\'22 Number of shorts has peaked 6 times between 251-271k between 2012-2020 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD slide has slowed in October, low only extended by 41 pips \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD likely to rebound from base 20-day Bollinger bands at 1.1486 \u8226\'20\'22 Worth hedging a range either 1.14-17 or 1.15-1.18 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders focus on a USD/JPY break above 114.00 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY, on Monday, saw a 111 point rise on EBS: biggest gain since Nov 2020 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has seen a 113.35-61 range on Wednesday, according to EBS prices \u8226\'20\'22 Spot reached 113.78, on Tuesday, the highest level in nearly 3 years \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY chart points to bigger gains above 114.00 \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importer bids are said to start at 113.00, right by Tuesday's low \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY versus EUR/JPY 30/60-day log correlations are both well above +0.50 GBP/USD holds close to 1.36 expiry level after UK GDP data \u8226\'20\'22 Cable remains close to 1.3600 after UK August GDP slightly below forecast \u8226\'20\'22 Up 0.4% vs +0.5% f/c. July GDP revised down to minus 0.1% \u8226\'20\'22 There is another 1.3600 option expiry for today's NY cut \u8226\'20\'22 Prior 1.3600 option expiry exerted magnetism over GBP/USD Tuesday \u8226\'20\'22 EU to unveil proposals aimed at resolving Brexit issues in NI \u8226\'20\'22 Speech from BoE's Cunliffe due at 1330GMT AUD/USD within strike of 0.7350 pre-U.S. inflation data \u8226\'20\'22 A large 0.7350 option expiry for the NY cut is helping to anchor AUD/USD \u8226\'20\'22 0.7330-0.7362 marks the intra-day range-to-date \u8226\'20\'22 0.7384 was Tuesday's one-month high, courtesy of more AUD/JPY buying \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. Sept CPI data due at 1230GMT, +5.3% YY f/c \u8226\'20\'22 'Patient' or 'aggresive'? Fed policymakers split on inflation response \u8226\'20\'22 Australian Sept jobs data due at 0030GMT, -137.5k f/c If just the Swiss were trading the franc, that would explain a lot Switzerland's franc is trading in a manner that would make sense if the only interest to trade it had origins in Switzerland. Switzerland's trade surplus has risen five-fold during a decade of super-easy monetary policy, regularly topping 5 billion francs per month this year, providing the franc with increasing support. In an environment that has supported big declines in the value of other funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the franc has gained 2.7% since March 2021 compared with a 6% drop for the yen and a 5% euro fall. This year the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has been quiet, with Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves growing just CHF 8 billion since March compared to CHF 167 bln growth in the previous 12 months which lifted EUR/CHF from 1.0500 to 1.1100. Since March 2021, EUR/CHF has dropped 1.1100-1.0700 with option vol now similar to that when EUR/CHF was pegged at 1.2000. It's possible that this year's modest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has merely countered a franc rise driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growing surplus. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3iXjpki"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3iXjpki}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3FJjCBG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3FJjCBG}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  options flag \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk from U.S. CPI and Fed minutes U.S. CPI and Fed minutes Wednesday can give more clarity to the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlook, so they carry a risk of increased \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, which is reflected in the price of overnight expiry (10-am New York Thursday) options. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility is unknown, so dealers use implied volatility to reflect expectations and determine the option premium before expiry, and that's higher since capturing the CPI and Fed minutes. However, while trading a premium to levels seen ahead of Wednesday's data risk, it's below levels seen before Friday's U.S. jobs data, except for USD/JPY, where the risk of actual volatility is clearly the greatest. Overnight expiry USD/JPY implied volatility gains over 2.0 to 10.0 since capturing Wednesday's data, which compares with 9.5 before NFP and is the highest since September FOMC, when it peaked at 11.25. The break-even for a simple vanilla straddle is now 47-JPY pips in either direction. EUR/USD overnight implied volatility peaked at 8.5 before U.S. jobs and was 10.75 before the September Fed - it's currently 7.5 - $36-pips in either direction. AUD/USD overnight implied volatility is 1.0 higher at 12.75, but was 14.0 before U.S. jobs and 17.0 before September's Fed. Current break-even is $39-pips in either direction. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3p5idzc"}