\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  resumed  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2  in second quarter - on a small scale\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 12:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Swiss reserve assets increased by 8.9 billion francs in Q2\par \par \uc2 * Increase shows the SNB trying to moderate franc strengthening\par \par \uc2 * Swiss Q2 current account surplus widens to 10.5 billion francs (Rewrites, adding analyst, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment)\par \par \uc2 By John Revill\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  appeared to increase its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the second quarter, although the level of purchases was far lower than a year earlier, when it was fighting to stop the Swiss franc from appreciating too much.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's reserve assets - which includes foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by the SNB - increased by 8.9 billion Swiss francs ($9.65 billion), higher than the 1.47 billion franc rise during the first quarter, according to balance of payments information published on Wednesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the figure was far lower than the 57.2 billion franc increase in reserve assets during the second quarter of 2020, as the SNB battled appreciation pressures on the safe-haven franc during the height of the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The SNB, which holds its quarterly monetary policy review on Thursday, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Analysts have said the reduced rate of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the SNB's more accepting attitude to the franc's strength, driven by inflows into Switzerland's defensively dominated stock market.\par \par \uc2 During the second quarter, non-residents spent 9.16 billion francs on Swiss equity and debt securities, driving demand and the valuation of the franc.\par \par \uc2 "From looking at the increase in reserve assets, the SNB appears to have tried to moderate this trend with increased \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Karsten Junius, an economist at \uc2 J.Safra Sarasin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SNB appears to have scaled back \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, with sight deposits that commercial banks hold at the SNB - another proxy for SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases - rising only by 3 billion francs during July and August, Junius added.\par \par \uc2 "It seems they now want to manage and slow the rise of the franc rather than block it with massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy seems to be able to cope with a nominally stronger franc, Junius added, with data on Wednesday showing the current account surplus widening to 10.51 billion francs during the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 The figure, up from a 3.2 billion franc surplus a year earlier, was mainly due to a higher receipts surplus in the goods trade, the SNB said.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.9242 Swiss francs) (Reporting by John Revill; Editing by Kevin Liffey and Catherine Evans) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-9-22T11:48:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210922eh9m011kv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
