\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Investors Bet on the Swiss Franc -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Caitlin Ostroff \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (October 6, 2020).\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has this year \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets the most since 2012, yet the franc keeps strengthening. That is a trajectory investors bet has further to go.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  last week said it has spent 90 billion Swiss francs ($98 billion) in the first half of the year to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating.\par \par \uc2 Still, the franc has gained 5.7% against the dollar this year and traded Monday at $1.0928 apiece. That is because the dollar has weakened: The WSJ Dollar Index, which tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of others, has declined 1.2% in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, speculative investors have increased bets that the Swiss franc will continue to strengthen against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be broadly viewed as a haven asset and tends to rally when investors grow risk-averse.\par \par \uc2 One reason investors are betting on appreciation: The stronger the franc gets against the dollar, the less likely Switzerland will draw criticism or sanctions from the U.S.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's coronavirus infection, the November election and other political uncertainties in the U.S. are weighing on the Trump administration and could help shield the Alpine nation from tariffs and other punitive actions in connection with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "You're unlikely to see aggressive moves against \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point in time," said Peter Kinsella, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Union Bancaire Priv\u233\'00\'E9e\uc2 . "The U.S. administration possibly has bigger fish to fry."\par \par \uc2 In previous years, Mr. Trump has frequently decried that other economies, including the eurozone and China, have gained the upper hand in trade by lowering interest rates, which allows their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken. A cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can make a nation's exports more competitive. Switzerland has historically managed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and been put on the U.S. watch list in the past.\par \par \uc2 Any action by the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  could prove to be merely symbolic, at least initially. Washington is more likely to consult with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to try to eliminate the unfair advantage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures have given a country, if it does anything at all.\par \par \uc2 But over the longer term, the U.S. could opt to levy tariffs against imports of drugs, precious metals, art or antiques from Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury uses three criteria to assess whether a country manipulates its exchange rate: active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, trade surpluses with the U.S., and a large country's current-account surplus, which is a broader measure of trade that includes investment income and other financial flows.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland was added to the Treasury's watch list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators in January, after it met the requirements on trade surplus and current-account surplus. At the time, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was less than 2% of its gross domestic product. But the first six months saw the SNB use an amount equivalent to more than 10% of its annual output of goods and services.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss data released last week marks the first time that quarterly figures were disclosed. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has historically only released annual data.\par \par \uc2 Investors expected the U.S. to release an updated report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators over the summer. One could still be forthcoming, they said. The Treasury didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 If the U.S. labeled Switzerland as a manipulator, the franc might briefly rally sharply against the dollar and euro, said \uc2 Luca Paolini\uc2 , chief strategist at \uc2 Pictet Asset Management\uc2 . But that would possibly prove to be a fleeting reaction: The SNB has previously told markets that the U.S. threat won't deter it from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The impact is going to be very short term," Mr. Paolini said. He expects the dollar to weaken, lending to the franc's strength.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury in August said Vietnam depressed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2019, providing the first test case for a Trump administration initiative to levy tariffs against countries for alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. On Friday, the U.S. said it would launch an investigation into Vietnam's actions in the same process it used to place tariffs on Chinese goods.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are motivated purely by monetary policy considerations," said Fabio Sonderer, a spokesperson for the SNB. "They are not aimed at conferring advantages on Switzerland by undervaluing the franc."\par \par \uc2 The strengthening franc makes Switzerland's imports cheaper and exports more expensive, weighing on its inflation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Sonderer also noted that Switzerland and the U.S. are "important economic partners" that are in frequent contact regarding financial and economic matters.\par \par \uc2 While the coming U.S. election may shield Switzerland from any immediate actions by the U.S., it may also serve to inhibit further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the SNB.\par \par \uc2 "With the elections coming up, you don't want to be seen doing anything, " said Mr. Paolini. He expects that the SNB will slow its steps for the rest of the year, and the Swiss franc will appreciate against the dollar. "It's a very volatile and uncertain situation."\par \par \uc2 In bond markets, the yield on the 10-year Treasury climbed to 0.76%, from 0.694% Friday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020201006ega6000de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
