\par\par \b \uc2 LIVE MARKETS-Speculation on the Swiss franc\b0\par\par\uc2 2,391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2020\par 15:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European shares rise\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 H&M\uc2  , STMicro deliver strong updates\par \par \uc2 * But Bayer sinks on impairment hit\par \par \uc2 * Wall St jumps at the opens Welcome to the home for real-time coverage of European equity markets brought to you by Reuters stocks reporters. You can share your thoughts with Joice Alves (joice.alves@thomsonreuters.com) and Julien Ponthus (julien.ponthus@thomsonreuters.com) in London and Stefano Rebaudo (stefano.rebaudo@thomsonreuters.com) and Danilo Masoni (danilo.masoni@thomsonreuters.com) in Milan.\par \par \uc2 SPECULATION ON THE SWISS FRANC (1335 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Expectations are often a lot more powerful than facts in financial markets and this might be the case for the Swiss Franc in the near future.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  spent 51.5 billion Swiss francs ($55.9 billion) in the second quarter on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the safe-haven Swiss franc's strength, up from 38.51 billion in the first quarter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said yesterday under its new policy of releasing data quarterly.\par \par \uc2 Now according to \uc2 ING Bank\uc2  analysts, while \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cautumn report is unlikely to be published before the U.S. election,\u8221\'20\'1D the risk of Switzerland being labelled as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator by the U.S. might generate \u8220\'20\'1Cfresh CHF speculative buying.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In January 2020 the Swiss franc hit its strongest against the euro in almost three years after the U.S. added Switzerland to its watch-list.\par \par \uc2 ING recalls that U.S. criteria on this matter include a trade account surplus as well as significant and one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite normally disclosing the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  once a year in the Annual Report, the \uc2 Swiss national bank\uc2  announced that it engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth an amount that narrowly exceeds 13% of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s GDP\u8221\'20\'1D in H1 2020, an ING research note says.\par \par \uc2 But we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t forget that expectations of more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the SNB can trigger the opposite effect, keeping the bullish mood on the Swiss Franc in check.\par \par \uc2 Early this year the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  cited continued concerns about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices of eight countries - Germany, Ireland, Italy, Japan, Malaysia, Singapore, South Korea and Vietnam - and added a ninth, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to its list.\par \par \uc2 The SNB denied its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were intended to give \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  a trading advantage.\par \par \uc2 In the chart below the recent progressive depreciation of the Swiss Franc against the dollar while the euro dollar was rising.\par \par \uc2 (Stefano Rebaudo)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 TALK OF THE TOWN: CONTESTED U.S. ELECTION RESULT RISK (1054 GMT)\par \par \uc2 There's been a lot of chatter about the risk of a "contested" election result in the U.S., especially after Trump refused to commit to accepting the result (of course, if he lost the election).\par \par \uc2 By the way, what does "contested" really mean?\par \par \uc2 When analysts talk of a contested result, this could mean anything from results being too close to call or taking longer than usual to count, to recounts, litigation, Supreme Court involvement, or even a more extreme scenario of increased civil unrest if Trump refuses to accept a Biden victory.\par \par \uc2 Markets hate uncertainty and some banks have already warned about volatile moves in the immediate aftermath of the Nov. 3 election. In fact, options activity (VIX etc) implies jumpy markets until December.\par \par \uc2 But some are sceptical about the likelihood of any prolonged impact. In a note to clients, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  dismissed the risk as \u8220\'20\'1Cnonsense\u8221\'20\'1D and Mizuho\u8217\'20\'19s head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales for financial institutions, Neil Jones, said that even a stalemate was unlikely to result in more risk-averse moves.\par \par \uc2 Tom McLoughlin, \uc2 UBS\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s head of fixed income for the Americas, said that the fact that markets did not react significantly to Trump\u8217\'20\'19s comments on the subject during the presidential debate suggests that they have already acknowledged and priced in the risk of a contested outcome.\par \par \uc2 McLoughlin said that Trump is likely to start legal action if he loses, but that the uncertainty is likely to be temporary. He advises clients to sit tight, noting that equity markets soon retraced their losses when the election result was contested in 2000.\par \par \uc2 "It makes sense for individual private investors to avoid restructuring completely their portfolios just to accommodate this period of time, which we think will be short-lived," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Elizabeth Howcroft)\par \par \uc2 *****\par \par \uc2 LAGARDERE\uc2  SURGES 14%: ANY TIP ANYONE? (1212 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Shares in Lagardere are clearly this session's sensation: they're up about 14%, surfing on their highest level since June 2019 but there's just a little problem here: no one appears to know why!\par \par \uc2 "It's crazy", a Paris-based sales trader just told us, noting that there was absolutely nothing in the news flow to justify such a move.\par \par \uc2 Volumes are already above their 90 day average, showing there's clearly more to the move than a very fat finger, he added.\par \par \uc2 Loss-making Lagardere is one of the most unlikely winner of the post COVID19 market crash. Lagardere, which owns a chain of shops in airports and train stations, had been hammered when COVID-19 travel restrictions had hit the travel and leisure sector.\par \par \uc2 Yet, its shares have been multiplied by about 3 since March with the company becoming a tug-of-war between some of France's most powerful tycoons fighting for its control.\par \par \uc2 Possible plans to shake-up the corporate structure of Lagardere and making it easier to it take over, oust \uc2 Arnaud Lagardere\uc2  or even break it up have ignited a speculation frenzy.\par \par \uc2 With its key holdings in the French press, it is also seen as a strategic asset in the French political arena, particularly ahead of the presidential elections of 2022.\par \par \uc2 Read our latest story on this French corporate saga here:\par \par \uc2 French billionaire Arnault builds up Lagardere holding\par \par \uc2 Here's Lagardere share performance in the last year and how it sky-rocketed close to 200% since March:\par \par \uc2 (Julien Ponthus, Danilo Masoni and Gwenaelle Barzic)\par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par OCADO: THE SELL-SIDE ISN'T KEEPING UP (1035 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  has crossed a big benchmark by overtaking \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  's in terms of market cap and if it keeps up rising on that rate, it sure seems like it will soon go over Ahold Delhaize to take the crown as the biggest listed European supermarket retailer!\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, a lot has been said about \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  not being your average retailer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  carries tech-level valuations, and has been touted as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  of retail", wrote \f28 \uc2 \f2 Neil Wilson\f28 \uc2 \f2  of Markets.com about two weeks ago when its results were published.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The online retailer has built-up a strong momentum thanks to the lockdown, its distribution deal with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Marks & Spencer\f28 \uc2 \f2  , its international expansion and the popularity of tech, which is the ultimate play of the post COVID-19 market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One thing though which could pour some cold water on the frenzy surrounding the stock is that sell side analysts are not keeping up with the share price surge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The median target price for the stock is 2,300 pence while the shares are currently trading above 2,800 pence, which is quite close to its all time high of 2,914 pence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More worryingly perhaps, according to I/B/E/S Refinitiv data, out of 18 sell-side analysts, only one has a target price (3,460p) about its current level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anyhow, here's how \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  has overtaken \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  and is getting closer to Ahold Delhaize:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Here's some reading:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  buoyed by online delivery demand and \f28 \uc2 \f2 M&S\f28 \uc2 \f2  switchover:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  ropes in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rolls-Royce\f28 \uc2 \f2  executive to replace long-time CFO:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Online grocery's share of UK market set to double, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ocado\f28 \uc2 \f2  says: [nL5N2EL14Y)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Julien Ponthus and Thyagaraju Adinarayan)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par U.S. ELECTIONS? IMPLIED SPX VOLATILITY IS HIGHER IN DECEMBER (0957 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Very little has changed about the pricing of U.S. presidential election risk after the unruly and basically uneventful debate between the two candidates, Biden and Trump, according to analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the risk pricing to maintain its current profile, highlighting SPX December implied volatility is above November, with strikes ranging from 3100 (-7%) to 3800 (+13%).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Republican President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  has questioned the validity of mail-in votes, raising concerns that the results of one or more states will be decided in the courts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The legislation's Dec. 11 end date will require Congress to return to the government funding question after what is likely to be a bruising fight over whether to confirm Trump's third Supreme Court nominee, Amy Coney Barrett.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But what about the outcome of the elections.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  , \u8220\'20\'1CA split government may result in the most post-election day tension, although this is highly subjective and fluid.\u8221\'20\'1D Let\u8217\'20\'19s see in detail.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It\u8217\'20\'19s true that a Democrat sweep will be a challenge for equity fundamentals as higher corporate taxes and more regulatory overhang is expected but it will also reduce the chance of a protracted disagreement over the outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Status quo \u8220\'20\'1Ccould be tactical positive\u8221\'20\'1D given the amount of risk priced into markets, but it may still be contested.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Biden win with a Republican Senate suggests a tightly contested election while \u8220\'20\'1Cit might read positively over the medium term,\u8221\'20\'1D as major policy shift will be less likely along with \u8220\'20\'1Cdaily \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  /headline risks.\u8221\'20\'1D\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Stefano Rebaudo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par THE M&A CARRY TRADE EXPLAINED (0858 GMT)\par               \par After a record third quarter that saw $1 trillion deals globally, the outlook for M&A\par remains sound.\par               \par A recovering economy and possible good vaccine news that boosts CEOs' confidence will likely\par drive an acceleration over the coming quarters.\par               \par And the monetary backdrop also remains extremely favourable for companies to build up their\par cash buffers on the bond market and engage in the M&A carry trade, especially in Europe.\par               \par "The economics of debt-financed M&A look compelling. European companies can borrow at 1% in\par the bond market to buy an earnings yield of 6% in the equity market," Citi says.\par               \par Out of the 10 biggest recent deals, 7 of them were financed primarily via Cash/Debt, it\par notes.\par               \par In regional differences, Citi says the M&A carry trade is even more attractive in the UK and\par less so on pricey Wall Street.\par               \par               \par (Danilo Masoni)\par               \par *****\par               \par               \par               \par OPENING SNAPSHOT: \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2  , STMICRO AND BAYER  (0740 GMT)\par               \par European shares surged after Sweden's retailer giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2   and chipmaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 STMicroelectronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  's\par results beat forecast.\par               \par Hopes for a new stimulus package from the U.S. and data showing Spanish factory activity\par picked up in September also helped sentiment.\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having shrunk the previous month amid a resurgence of coronavirus infections, \f28 \uc2 \f2 IHS Markit\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) of Spanish manufacturing companies rose to 50.8 in September from 49.9 in August. A reading above 50 denotes growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pan European index is up 0.7% in early trade, touching a 10-day high, with retailers and banks leading the gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In terms of single stocks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares jumped 6.3% to the top of the STOXX and hitting its highest level since June after the company reported Q3 profit beat estimates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Paris index is outperforming the European market after chip maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 STMicroelectronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  said its preliminary Q3 net revenues were higher than forecast, at $2.67 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meantime, shares at Bayer sank 11.7%, touching its lowest point since March, as the drug maker announced plans for more cost cuts and said it will take impairment charges on its agricultural business amid low commodity prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Joice Alves)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ON OUR RADAR: STMICRO, ROLLS ROYCE, BAYER (0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures are pointing for European shares in positive territory at the opening as hopes for more U.S. stimulus measures are helping risk appetite.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the corporate world, semiconductor manufacturer \f28 \uc2 \f2 STMicroelectronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  reported preliminary Q3 revenue exceeded its expectations, boosted by a sharp rise in demand for automotive products and microcontrollers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britain's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rolls-Royce\f28 \uc2 \f2  would raise 2 billion pounds ($2.6 billion) in a rights issue, 1 billion through a bond offering and gain loans support to recapitalise its balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in Bayer are down 4.7% after the company announced plans for more cost cuts, impairment charges.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meantime, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. FDA\f28 \uc2 \f2  has broadened its investigation of a serious illness in \f28 \uc2 \f2 AstraZeneca Plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  's COVID-19 vaccine study and will look at data from earlier trials of similar vaccines developed by the same scientists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the M&A front, shares in Swiss contract drug maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lonza\f28 \uc2 \f2  are seen up 3% after the company launched the sale of its Lonza Specialty Ingredients (LSI) unit, which could be worth 3-3.5 billion Swiss francs ($3.25-3.79 billion).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French utility Engie moved closer to selling its stake in waste and water group Suez to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Veolia\f28 \uc2 \f2  after a 3.4 billion euro offer ($4 billion).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KLM, the Dutch arm of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air France-KLM SA\f28 \uc2 \f2  , has reached an agreement with its flight staff, excluding pilots, over cost cuts needed in return for state aid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 British retailer Halfords raised its first half profit outlook as it continued to benefit from a cycling boom during the coronavirus pandemic.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meantime more bad news for the battered banking sector from the U.S., where the FED will extend big bank buybacks, dividend curbs through end of year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Joice Alves, Danilo Masoni, Stefano Rebaudo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ******\par               \par               \par               \par               \par MORNING CALL: MORE STIMULUS, TOUGHER LOCKDOWN (0536 GMT)\par               \par European shares are seen in positive territory as hopes for more U.S. stimulus measures help\par risk appetite.\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. House of Representatives Speaker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nancy Pelosi\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Treasury Secretary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Steven Mnuchin\f28 \uc2 \f2  both said they hope for a breakthrough on a coronavirus relief package, as the House postponed a vote on a new $2.2 trillion Democratic coronavirus bill.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But growing uncertainty ahead of November's U.S. presidential election and rising coronavirus cases in Europe capped the optimism.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 British PM \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boris Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  urged people to obey rules imposed to tackle an accelerating second wave of the pandemic outbreak, adding that otherwise a tougher lockdown could be implemented.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also be watching EZ and UK manufacturing PMI data due to be out this morning at 8 and 8.30 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial spreadbetters at IG expect London's FTSE to open 25 points higher at 5,891, Frankfurt's DAX to open 56 points up at 12,817 and Paris' CAC to open 27 points higher at 4,830.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Joice Alves)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***** \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-10-1T14:35:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201001ega102uuv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
