\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss Franc Could Extend Gains, Forcing SNB to Act -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2020\par 13:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1142 GMT - The safe haven Swiss franc will likely appreciate further on global risk aversion, forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says. "Amid geopolitical risks, Brexit, potential euro-area complications and a generally fragile global economy, discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to remain the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  's instrument of choice," \uc2 BofA\uc2  analysts say. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come through an increase in sight deposits--money that commercial banks part overnight with the \uc2 SNB\uc2 , the analysts say. The \uc2 SNB\uc2  may also raise the exemption threshold for negative interest rates on sight deposits if franc appreciation pressures build, they say. EUR/CHF is last steady at 1.0805. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1141 GMT - Shares in U.K. house-builders gain after industry data showed house prices gathering momentum this month. Mortgage-lender Nationwide said annual price growth picked up to 5% in September, the highest rate since September 2016. Prices rose 0.9% month-on-month after taking account of seasonal factors, versus a 2% gain in August. \uc2 Taylor Wimpey\uc2  is the housing sector's biggest FTSE 100 riser, up 1.5%, followed by gains for \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  and \uc2 Barratt Developments\uc2  . Nationwide says pent-up demand is coming through and temporary tax relief is boosting momentum. "Behavioural shifts may also be boosting activity as people reassess their housing needs and preferences as a result of life in lockdown," says Nationwide's Chief Economist Robert Gardner.(philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1137 GMT - The gradual healing process of the German labor market continued in September, \uc2 Stefan Schilbe\uc2  , chief economist for Germany at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , says. Unemployment fell for the third month in a row in September and the small drop led to the first decline in the unemployment rate since March 2019. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects this trend to continue as companies will use current employees more intensively by lowering the usage of short-time working before embarking on big hiring programs. "This paints the picture of a gradual improvement rather than a quick return to normality," the economist says. It could take a few more months before unemployment starts to drop more markedly, Schilbe says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1028 GMT - German unemployment dropped by 108,000 in September, showing its best September performance since 2012 and suggesting that the negative impact from the Covid-19 crisis is gradually weakening, says \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  , global head of macro at ING. Currently, short-time work schemes and a relatively stable labour market are supportive for private consumption, he says. "However, don't forget that the labour market is no longer a natural stronghold of the economy but rather a stronghold supported by fiscal steroids," Brzeski says. Looking ahead, ongoing structural transitions in many sectors as well as the longer-term impact from social distancing and Covid-19 restrictions are likely to increase unemployment in the course of 2021, Brzeski says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1027 GMT - Employment and unemployment levels in Germany show the encouraging stabilization of the labor market, says Eckart Tuchtfeld, senior economist for German economic policy at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . In September, seasonally adjusted unemployment in Germany fell for the third consecutive month and employment increased for the second time. Tuchtfeld says the favorable picture is due to a large extent to the use of short-time work and that some of the current short-time workers run the risk of becoming unemployed later on, for example if a company that is no longer competitive becomes insolvent. But provided that the coronavirus pandemic doesn't worsen significantly, the slight upward trend on the labor market is likely to continue for several months. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT - The German economic recovery led to an improved labour market situation in the summer, \uc2 Fritzi Koehler-Geib\uc2  , chief economist at \uc2 KfW\uc2 , says. The feared rise in unemployment to above the three million mark appears to have been averted for the time being but she says a rise in infections could lead to a slowdown in the recovery. "Setbacks are also possible if stricter government regulations on social distancing become necessary in Germany and Europe and consumers spend less out of fear of infection," she says. Next year, the shortage of skilled workers may become a barrier to economic recovery, she warns. "It is crucial for the recovery that short-time workers and the unemployed receive training in line with market requirements," the economist says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0946 GMT - \uc2 Amundi Asset Management\uc2  is "constructive" on euro peripheral government debt but is no longer positive on Italian 10-year government bonds, or BTPs, and considers there is better protection in five-year BTPs, says Matteo Germano, head of multi-asset. \uc2 Amundi\uc2 's positive view on peripheral debt is due to support from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  , favorable technical factors and the EU Recovery Fund, he says. However, hedging upside risks related to reflation, vaccine availability and stronger growth is a "prudent" step, he says. In this context, \uc2 Amundi\uc2  believes that five-year BTPs could offer better protection than 10-year BTPs "as this would support less if a bear steepening of curves materialises in Europe," Germano says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT - The South African rand edges higher after data showed inflation eased slightly in August, driven by lower food prices. Consumer prices fell by 3.1% year-on-year in August after a 3.2% rise in July, but remained within the \uc2 South African \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's target range of 3-6%. "Despite the weakness of inflation, further monetary easing is unlikely," Capital Economics economist Virag Forizs says. "Policymakers at the Reserve Bank left their repo rate unchanged at 3.50% at their meeting earlier this month and the communications were notably less dovish." USD/ZAR falls to 16.9152 from 16.9602 before the data and is down 0.2% on the day. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0943 GMT - The economic recovery, coupled with fiscal and monetary support, prompts AXA Investments to remain overweight in equities, investment strategist Franz Wenzel says in a webinar. "We remain overweight in risky papers, pro-equity," he says, adding that AXA IM is investing in growth equities. The backdrop is the economic recovery, monetary policy support from central banks and fiscal stimulus by governments, he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT - AXA Investment Managers "wouldn't be surprised" if the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announces an expansion and extension of the EUR1.35 trillion Pandemic Emergency Purchase Programme, investment strategist Franz Wenzel says in a webinar. The current program runs until end-June 2021. AXA IM looks for an increase of the asset purchase volume by up to EUR500 billion, Wenzel says. AXA IM isn't alone with its expectations of a bigger and longer PEPP program, as many market participants look for an announcement about the program increased at the \uc2 ECB\uc2  's December meeting. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT - The narrowing of the U.K.'s current account deficit in the second quarter is welcome news but it is likely to widen again as domestic demand recovers, EY Item Club says. "The current account deficit has long been considered a potential source of vulnerability for the U.K. economy--particularly if there were to be any major loss of investor confidence in the U.K. for any reason," \uc2 EY\uc2  economist \uc2 Howard Archer\uc2  says. The deficit narrowed to GBP2.8 billion in the second quarter from GBP20.8 billion in the first quarter as imports fell more than exports on weaker domestic demand. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT - The Swiss KOF Barometer, a composite leading indicator of the country's GDP growth, increased in September for the fourth straight month and reached a decade-high at 113.8 points. "At present, the economy is taking a 'V-\u8203\'20\'0Bshaped' course, so that a recovery of the Swiss economy can be expected for the time being," the KOF Economic Institute says, warning that a second wave of Covid-19 cases could lead to a sharp revision of this assessment. "Data confirm that the Swiss economy is in a pretty good place and the risks to our GDP forecast for 3Q are on the upside," David Oxley, senior Europe economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 30, 2020 07:42 ET (11:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200930eg9u001hn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
