esterday rising from 1.1250 to 1.1302 (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?) \u8226\'20\'22 Pair subsequently sliding to 1.1224 (rebalancing?) \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 SNB is already one of the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  players, maybe the biggest player \u8226\'20\'22 Bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks greater distortion. To step back risks disorder Dollar hemmed in versus yen as U.S. coronavirus surge weighs \u8226\'20\'22 Asian shares up as U.S., China recoveries gather pace \u8226\'20\'22 Though dollar hemmed in as a resurgence dulls \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk-taking \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has seen tight trading Friday, 107.44-57 range on EBS so far \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY "bull trap" above key Fibo keeps a lid on market \u8226\'20\'22 Trap set above the 107.97 Fibo -- a 50% retrace of the 109.85-106.08 drop \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 30/60-day correlations with USD/JPY are strong, readings +0.64/+0.61 GBP/USD tests two-day low after UK PM addresses \uc2 EU\uc2  issue \u8226\'20\'22 Cable eased to test 1.2440 after UK PM Johnson addressed big EU-UK issue \u8226\'20\'22 Johnson: Other good options if we can't reach deal with \uc2 EU\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 1.2440 was two-day low after French political news \u8226\'20\'22 1.2470 was rally high from the 0740GMT 1.2440 low \u8226\'20\'22 1.2485 was early Ldn high. UK June final service PMI 47.1 vs 47.0 f/c \u8226\'20\'22 EU-UK trade talks resume next week AUD/USD-Poised for rally as positives pile up \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD trades in well supported but narrow 0.6914-0.6932 range in Asia Fri \u8226\'20\'22 Bolstered by rally in metals, commodities & stocks; \uc2 ASX\uc2  200 +2.2% this week \u8226\'20\'22 Australia May retail sales see record surge as economy reopens \u8226\'20\'22 Robust U.S. jobs data, stellar Caixin China services PMI boost risk mood \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. holiday dampens trading activity but rally likely to resume next week \u8226\'20\'22 Global surge in new virus cases taken in stride; U.S. at record \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance 0.6950-55, 0.6974-77, support 0.6900-05, 0.6875-80 The ZAR is sitting on a fiscal time bomb External factors continue to underpin the South African rand but investors won't be able to ignore the enormous fiscal strains building within the country for ever. Positive rhetoric from the U.S. Federal Reserves's \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  earlier in the week helped put a shine on the ZAR and it has firmed to levels above 17.00 versus the USD. Increasing investor optimism about global growth driven by the potential for a faster than expected U.S. recovery will help underpin risk sentiment but it will not defuse the massive fiscal time bomb threatening South Africa's credit ratings. The country faces significant rating downgrades as its government debt projections head skywards. South Africa is caught in a debt trap, it cannot spend its way out of recession without increasing the debt burden and if investors start worrying about potential debt default, appetite for the ZAR's high yield will be tested. In the short-term the ZAR will draw strength from the easing of global coronavirus lockdowns and a more positive slant to U.S. data but the fiscal outlook will continue to apply the brakes. USD/ZAR daily candle chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2CXDncC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2CXDncC}}}\uc2  SNB's sledgehammer may crack its own nut The massive size of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be its eventual undoing. \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold between 53 billion and 100 billion francs ($56-$105.7 billion) in a few months and the impact of those flows is evident in major \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets where Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a dominant force. Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes may be measured in trillions but there are very few players that trade billion-dollar tickets let alone do that on a daily basis. Such huge sizes require liquidity that is rarely available and in the middle of a crisis there is even less chance that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  (SNB) will find the liquidity necessary to meet its requirements. But there is another and potentially bigger issue. The SNB rebalances \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , converting a big proportion into dollars. To do that the SNB must sell EUR/USD after buying EUR/CHF. Effectively the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supporting the cross then selling back to itself. The greater its dominance the more it is effectively dealing with itself. Trapped within this vicious circle the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will decrease, while the risk of market disorder grows. EUR/CHF and Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3ir0JY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3ir0JY}}}\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-7-6T10:42:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200703eg7301lpt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
