\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  signals need to maintain negative rates\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2020\par 13:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Keeps expansive policy in place as expected\par \par \uc2 * Signals current policy needed for "some time"\par \par \uc2 * SNB says "no limit" to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Rewrites, adding comments, analysts, market reaction)\par \par \uc2 By John Revill and Silke Koltrowitz\par \par \uc2 BERN, June 18 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will keep its ultra-expansive monetary policy for "some time" after cutting its inflation outlook and saying it needs negative rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shield \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the coronavirus driven-recession.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing its sharpest downturn in decades after the pandemic shut down many businesses, pushed up unemployment and dented demand at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 The SNB expects Swiss GDP to shrink 6% this year, the worst downturn since the aftermath of oil price shocks in the 1970s, and sees no rise in consumer prices before 2022, and then by just 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the SNB kept its policy interest rate and the rate it charges on sight deposits at minus 0.75%, as unanimously expected by analysts polled by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "I believe it will be necessary to maintain this expansionary monetary policy for some time," SNB Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "We need a completely different outlook before we can change monetary policy, more inflation and also a much better economic outlook," he told journalists.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc weakened slightly against the euro after the decision.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's new conditional inflation forecast of below 2% over the entire forecast horizon, suggests it will hold the policy rate until at least the first quarter of 2023, said Credit Suisse economist Maxime Botteron.\par \par \uc2 At EFG Bank, economist GianLuigi Mandruzzato also said he did not see rates, which have been negative since 2014, rising until 2023 unless the recovery was much stronger than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB maintains a clear dovish bias, leaving the door open to further accommodation, but not necessarily more negative interest rates, particularly if the risk of new (COVID-19) infections crystallises," he said.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, analysts expect the SNB to keep restraining the Swiss franc mainly through increased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The safe-haven franc rose to its highest level against the euro in nearly five years last month, prompting the SNB to ramp up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases in order to protect the export-orientated economy.\par \par \uc2 Sight deposits - a proxy for SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have risen by nearly 100 billion Swiss francs ($105 billion) this year, dwarfing \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the whole of 2018 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 Jordan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had made "substantial \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in recent weeks, and could continue to do so if necessary to maintain reasonable financial and monetary conditions.\par \par \uc2 "There is no specific limit ... we are forced to do that to bring inflation back over time to the range of price stability."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.9490 Swiss francs) (Reporting by John Revill; Editing by Toby Chopra, Kirsten Donovan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-6-18T12:16:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200618eg6i015wf\par }\page {\par\fs20{