\par\par \b \uc2 SNB\uc2  Maintains  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  as Swiss Franc Gains -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 17:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1254 ET - Investors reached for safe assets Monday, pushing the Swiss franc up 1.2% against the U.S. dollar, but it could be much stronger without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As of Friday, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has likely spent about 2% of its gross domestic product on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, according to a \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  research report. One franc buys $1.0794, or EUR0.9419. "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think we would have seen parity [with the euro] already," said Nikolay Markov, a senior economist at \uc2 Pictet Asset Management\uc2 . The franc's increase has put the \uc2 SNB\uc2  in a tough spot as the Treasury recently added Switzerland to a watchlist of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to weigh the potential label with its need to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation and counter low inflation. (caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1223 ET - Bank of America Global Research says the ratio of the S&P 500 dividend yield over the 10-year Treasury yield has jumped to 2.5x, during the current cycle--overshooting some recent spikes. Analysts say the prior three peaks were followed by the S&P 500 outperforming the 10-year Treasury by 31 percentage points on average over the next 12 months. Since 1951 when the ratio was this high, \uc2 BofA\uc2  says, stocks have returned 19 times more than Treasurys. But \uc2 BofA\uc2  warns while the energy sector currently offers the highest dividend yield at 5.1%, "given continued risk to earnings, we recommend 'safer' yield sectors, such as Utilities (3.2%)." (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1134 ET - Canada's budget planning process for 2020 is likely in upheaval with the unexpected plunge in oil prices. On Friday, Finance Minister \uc2 Bill Morneau\uc2  said he was prepared to inject fiscal stimulus as required, and would book a larger risk contingency in the coming 2020 budget plan to account for uncertainty related to the coronavirus epidemic. The 17% plunge to price in west Texas intermediate, to the $34 range, represents a fresh body blow to budget planning. Canadian crude from western Canada already trades at a sizable discount to WTI, due to pipeline bottlenecks among other things. Morneau has not provided a date at which time he will present his budget plan, which originally was to have a climate-change focus. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1111 ET - Slovakia, Slovenia and Malta are the eurozone's most exposed countries to supply-chain disruptions caused by the coronavirus. A report from ING examines the value chain of each eurozone economy in terms of overall exposure to China, South Korea and Italy, and concludes that those three nations have a higher chance of being affected as they depend more on countries worst hit by the epidemic. On the upside, the Netherlands, Latvia and Portugal are the least exposed and big economies such as Germany, France and Spain are in the mid-range. By sector, textiles, electronics and electrical equipment bear the highest risk, according to ING's ranking. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 ET - The dual impact of Covid-19 and the significant oil price shock will put pressure on some sovereign credit fundamentals and potentially ratings, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. In developed markets, the key drivers will be the effect on growth, if it persists, and the fiscal and monetary responses, the ratings firm says. Emerging markets face additional risks related to commodity export receipts, capital flows and exchange-rate pressures, it adds. Low oil prices weighed on ratings of major exporters in 2014-16 and will do so again if the oil price war leads to sustained lower prices, says \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  , after oil prices had their biggest drop in nearly three decades after Saudi Arabia said on Sunday it would increase production and cut prices after talks with Russia on production cuts collapsed. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The worst for the global economy is yet to come over the next several months, says \uc2 Joachim Fels\uc2  , global economic advisor at PIMCO. Fels says China's manufacturing and demand slump will affect activity in the rest of the world and demand for services will contract due to containment measures and fear. PIMCO expects the spread of the coronavirus outside China to peak in the next few months and foresees a U-shaped trajectory for global growth over the next few quarters. "However, this 'U' may feel initially like an 'I' as economic growth drops sharply, then like an 'L' as the economy finds a bottom but visibility remains low for some time, before growth inflects higher and the upward bend in the 'U' materializes," Fels says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1026 ET - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects a rebound in French and Italian production data from January, to be published on Tuesday. For France, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  forecasts a 1.5% rise in industrial production following a 2.8% contraction in December, reflecting the dissipation of disruptions related to strikes against pension reform. This forecast is below consensus because some recent economic releases indicate that disruptions to activity in some sectors were still observed in early January, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. In the case of Italy, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects a partial rebound after a sharp contraction in December. However, he bank says it won't be a sharp increase, as some of the decline seen in December was of a temporary nature and is unlikely to fully reverse. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1001 ET - The U.K.'s decision to leave the European Aviation Safety Authority lacks ambition and could threaten jobs as well as inward investment, says \uc2 ADS Group\uc2  , an aerospace and defense industry lobby group. ADS--which represents more than 1,100 U.K. companies--says continued participation in \uc2 EASA\uc2  is the best option to maintain global export-market access as well as the competitiveness of the U.K.'s GBP36 billion aerospace industry. "U.K. influence in \uc2 EASA\uc2  contributes to raising standards in global aviation, supports collaboration with our international partners and helps make our industry attractive to the investment it needs," ADS Chief Executive \uc2 Paul Everitt\uc2  says. "We're disappointed [the government] hasn't taken a more ambitious approach. It's essential it works with us to achieve a regime that doesn't put jobs at risk." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0951 ET - "To respond to the price movement is the one thing you don't want to do." That is what \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  Chief US Equity Strategist Jonathan Golub tells WSJ he is advising clients this morning as stocks fall sharply amid worries about economic growth, profits and the spread of the coronavirus. For the year, the bank now expects weaker gains in the S&P 500, but \uc2 Golub\uc2  anticipates metrics like the yield on 10-year treasury notes, stock multiples and earnings to return to more normal levels by the end of the year. (micah.maidenberg@wsj.com; @MicahMaidenberg)\par \par \uc2 0937 ET - \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the deposit rate by 10 basis points to minus 0.60% at Thursday's meeting, with risks of a 20-basis-point move, but it sticks to the view that easing monetary policy will do little to shield the eurozone economy from the impact of Covid-19. The cut should be accompanied by an increase in the multiplier for the tiered reserves to eight from six, say \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's chief European economist \uc2 Simon Wells\uc2  and senior economist \uc2 Fabio Balboni\uc2  . The economists add that more QE is possible--it's currently EUR20 billion a month--, "but the political hurdle is higher and sovereign yields are at all-time lows for most countries," and therefore they expect the volume to remain unchanged. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 ET - The EU is unlikely to take a big economic hit from the U.K.'s exit, says \uc2 RBC Wealth Management\uc2  . If the EU loses access to the U.K. market due to the potential breakdown of negotiations over a new trade agreement, the impact would be modest as EU exports to the U.K. represent only 3% of EU GDP, \uc2 RBC\uc2  says. "Moreover, the damage would be partially offset by some diversion of trade, investment and skilled migrants away from the U.K. to the EU ," says \uc2 RBC\uc2 's Frederique Carrier. Brexit seems to have boosted support for the EU among its 27 remaining members and the loss of the U.K.'s contribution to the bloc's economy and budget should be manageable, he adds. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 ET - Against a backdrop of rising pressure on major central banks to loosen policy in response to coronavirus, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  now expects the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  to cut its repo rate back into negative territory at its April 28 decision, if not before. "However, as in other countries, any monetary response to the virus is likely to come alongside more targeted measures on the fiscal and regulatory fronts." Capital Economics notes that it's possible the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  may start to buy corporate bonds, but the recent bond market rally has weakened the case for additional purchases of government debt. "Meanwhile, although mortgage-backed bonds offer a huge stock of assets to buy, we doubt that the bank will go there for fear of being seen as stoking the housing market." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 09, 2020 12:54 ET (16:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200309eg39004qv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
