\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss Franc's Rise Has  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  in Bind -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Caitlin Ostroff and Paul J. Davies \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (February 24, 2020).\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc has climbed to its highest level against the euro in more than four years, leaving Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a dilemma: Do nothing and potentially damage the economy, or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail the rise and risk angering the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has so far kept mum on its intentions and investors are torn. Some seem convinced the franc will keep rising: Hedge funds betting on further strengthening have increased their net long positions since the start of the year, according to data on futures markets from the \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2 . Others warn such a gamble is foolhardy.\par \par \uc2 "If you try to play games with the SNB, it's very dangerous for you," said Thomas Stucki, chief investment officer at St. Galler Kantonalbank and former head of asset management at the SNB. "They will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market."\par \par \uc2 Weekly data from the SNB, which analysts monitor as a proxy for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggest it has been acting to stem the franc's rise already this year, buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and selling the franc. Its efforts have so far failed to hit its main target: The franc has risen more than 2% against the euro since the end of 2019 to EUR0.94.\par \par \uc2 "If they don't really manage to turn the tide on the current trend, then they could risk having to throw in the towel," said Andreas Steno Larsen, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income strategist at \uc2 Nordea Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the franc has fallen more than 1% against the dollar to $1.02. This is a problem because the euro matters more for Swiss trade and inflation, but the dollar will matter more to the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , which last month added Switzerland to its watch list of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators.\par \par \uc2 The franc is nearly 7.5% below its fair value to the dollar, similar to the Chinese yuan, according to \uc2 Steven Englander\uc2 , head of global G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research and North America macro strategy at \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A strong franc is a problem for the SNB because it makes imports cheaper and lowers inflation, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to stoke with some of the lowest interest rates in the world. However, a weaker franc makes exports cheaper to the U.S., its second-biggest market. That risks attracting the ire of President Trump, who has been vocal in his views that the strength of the dollar puts the U.S. at an unfair disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 Washington judges \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation on three criteria: the size of a country's trade surplus with the U.S., the size of its overall current account surplus and how much it has spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Treasury department has said \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  met the first two criteria.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland might not have a lot of wiggle room before it meets the third criteria. Investors pushed the franc higher after the Treasury's report, speculating the SNB might be more hesitant to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at bay.\par \par \uc2 The main proxy for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the SNB's weekly report on sight deposits, or short-term franc deposits held at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by domestic banks, the government and others. When these are growing, that is a sign that the SNB is selling francs, because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banks and others, who would end up holding more francs in their deposits.\par \par \uc2 Up until mid-February, these sight deposits grew by 5.5 billion francs ($5.6 billion).\par \par \uc2 The U.S. rule is that a country shouldn't spend more than 2% of GDP on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; with Swiss GDP this year forecast to be about 700 billion francs, that suggests it can spend up to 14 billion francs on holding back its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Simple math suggests it might be able to spend less than 9 billion francs over the rest of 2020 before crossing the Treasury's line in the sand.\par \par \uc2 The SNB appeared to launch a big \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last summer: Sight deposits rose by 13.5 billion francs between mid-July and mid-September, then stopped. This was roughly equivalent to 2% of GDP, said Paul Meggyesi, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 . in London.\par \par \uc2 "I don't agree with the strand of thought that it doesn't matter if Switzerland is labeled a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator," Mr. Meggyesi said. "This is a U.S. administration that is prepared to use the tools at its disposal to counteract what it sees as unfair trade relations."\par \par \uc2 It is uncertain exactly what being labeled a manipulator would mean for Switzerland. The SNB has spent much more than 2% of GDP on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past and has been on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch list before. However, Mr. Trump has given American companies the power to pursue tariffs against foreign competitors who have benefited from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in their countries.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's problem continues to grow because it exports more than it imports, and attracts a lot of capital that is looking for a safe home.\par \par \uc2 These pressures eased while the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was raising interest rates in 2018 and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also looked like it might start unwinding its easy money policies. That ended last year, when both central banks reversed course amid concerns about global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The SNB could make it more costly to hold money in Swiss banks by taking rates further below the current minus 0.75% level, which is matched only by Denmark. It might have to if the \uc2 ECB\uc2  goes deeper into subzero territory, said David Oxley, senior Europe economist at Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB is focused on maintaining that gap between European interest rates and their own," Mr. Oxley said. "They're kind of between a rock and a hard place."\par \par \uc2 Write to Caitlin Ostroff at caitlin.ostroff@wsj.com and Paul J. Davies at paul.davies@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2020 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200224eg2o0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
