\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss Franc Rises on Virus Fears, US Pressure on SNB -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2020\par 12:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1108 GMT - The Swiss franc rises to its strongest level against the euro since April 2017 as investors turn to the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to fears about the spread of the coronavirus, with EUR/CHF hitting a low of 1.0687. Investors have further encouragement from U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Swiss franc to limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. "U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down has given the Swiss franc bulls another reason to be long," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's Kit Juckes. The U.S. added Switzerland to the Treasury's watch list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators earlier in the month as a warning to the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  . Speaking to Bloomberg at Davos, \uc2 SNB\uc2 's chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  denied the \uc2 SNB\uc2  was engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1058 GMT - January's ifo index reading shows that for the time being, the economy remains stuck at the bottom, says \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  , chief economist at ING in Germany. The first drop of the ifo index since August 2019 is "a cold shower for all optimists," Brzeski says. According to Brzeski, the decline to 95.9 shows that the German economy, particularly the manufacturing sector, has still not bottomed out. "While the service sector has already gone through a real rebound since the summer, the manufacturing sector still looks paralysed, suffering from trade conflicts and the global manufacturing downturn," Brzeski says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1034 GMT - The decline in Germany's ifo business climate index for January comes as a bit of a surprise, says Andrew Kenningham, chief Europe economist for \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . The ifo business climate index came in at 95.9 points in January, below the 97.5 forecasted by \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . "This sounds a warning note for those expecting an impressive rebound in the German growth rate," Kenningham says. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  expects the German economy to grow "at only a very anemic pace" in the first half of this year. While the manufacturing component of ifo rose, the services component fell. Kenningham says this is in line with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  ' forecast of an improvement in the export-oriented manufacturing sector coinciding with a weakening of domestic demand. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is unlikely to restart bond purchases unless it brings interest rates, currently at 0.75%, to zero, says Roberto Mialich of \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . Therefore, the BOE is unlikely to mention the possibility of quantitative easing at this Thursday's policy meeting. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates by 25 basis points to 0.5% on Thursday and its view is that the BOE will be more aggressive in loosening monetary policy than what the market expects but "not to the point to negative interest rates," adds Mialich. The 10-year gilt yield is last down almost 4 basis points to 0.530%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT - Many market watchers neglect the risk of higher inflation, which could be a risk for bond markets in 2020, says Werner Kraemer, senior economic analyst at \uc2 Lazard Asset Management\uc2 . "Monetary expansion is entering its twelfth year, full employment prevails in many countries, wage costs are rising slightly, the state and the associated costs are expanding more and more, globalization is being increasingly restricted and the oil price is moving slowly but steadily upwards," he says. The rising gold price in recent years could also be an early indicator of inflation risks, he adds. "Therefore, it cannot be completely ruled out that the bond market, and thus the stock market, will run into difficulties again due to rising inflation rates," says Kraemer. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT - Rising sea levels are set to bruise the gross domestic product of G20 countries due to coastal flooding, even if they take ambitious action to halt climate change, says a study from the \uc2 International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis\uc2  . IIASA projects that under two scenarios--one below 2 degrees Celsius of warming by 2100 and another overshooting the target--annual global economy-wide losses could hit more than 4%. "This confirms the importance and economic efficiency of adaptation in the long term: Making sure that coastal communities and their infrastructure are climate-resilient will affect economies across the globe much less than persistent climate impacts in the absence of climate action," says study author Thomas Schinko. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 1016 GMT - Data from the \uc2 ifo Institute\uc2  shows that the mood among German companies is somewhat gloomier again, says Friederike Koehler-Geib, chief economist at KfW Group. Although the fall in the ifo business climate index came as a surprise, Koehler-Geib says there are reasons for it. The agreement between China and the U.S., as well as the orderly withdrawal of the U.K. from the EU "merely eliminate the immediate downside risks, but they don't yet create any real stimulus for the economy," Koehler-Geib says. In Germany, Koehler-Geib says an end to the industrial recession "is only dimly visible," while construction and services are showing signs of fatigue at the beginning of the year. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0950 GMT - The euro falls to an eight-week low of 1.1016 against the U.S. dollar on weaker-than-expected German Ifo Business Climate Index for January. The data came in at 95.9, down from 96.3 in December and expectations of around to 97, according to a WSJ poll of economists. "Sentiment among German managers is somewhat diminished" as companies are "more pessimistic outlook for the coming months," says \uc2 Clemens Fuest\uc2  , president of the \uc2 ifo Institute\uc2 . The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is last at 1.1021, having fallen to an eight-week low of 1.1017 after the announcement from 1.1029 beforehand, according to FactSet.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT - The regional elections in Italy will make an early parliamentary election in the country less likely, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. The results in the Emilia-Romagna region showed a "surprisingly strong" performance by the center-left, led by the Democratic Party. The results could strengthen the position of the party, which is part of the ruling coalition in Rome, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. "All in all, this result should push back the risk of an early election," the Swiss bank says. (pietro.lombardi@dowjones.com; @pietrolombard10)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT - Italy's regional elections results remove risks of a government crisis in the short term, but a number of factors still poses a threat to the ruling coalition's future, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2  . The victory of a centre-left candidate in Emilia Romagna allows the Democratic party to claim victory on both local and national fronts, the bank says, representing an opportunity for the PD-MS5 coalition to reaffirm the longevity of its government. However, "many hurdles persist," according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2  , including a fractured Five-Star Movement, more regional elections and a referendum to slash the number of seats in parliament at the horizon. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT - Tender offers for Japanese companies reached Y2.1 trillion ($19.22 billion) in 2019, the highest level since the record Y2.6 trillion set in 2007, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says in a note. While tender offers to buy out subsidiaries made up the lion's share last year, hostile takeovers, once thought difficult to achieve in Japan, have also been observed, Goldman analysts say. "We expect to see more tender offers going forward, given the resistance to hostile takeovers has started fading among the Japan corporates."(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT - A 25 basis-point reduction in interest rates to 0.5% by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  on Thursday wouldn't move much sterling as one rate cut this year is already fully priced in by the market, says Roberto Mialich, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . One rate cut is widely expected, although a second interest rate cut is "questionable," he says. Interest rate futures show a 32% probability of a second rate cut this year, he says. The pound is flat against the U.S. dollar at 1.3074, and also against the euro at 0.8434. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2020 06:08 ET (11:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200127eg1r000j6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
