\par\par \b \uc2 German Ifo Index Worth a Second Glance: \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2020\par 13:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1211 GMT - Although the Ifo business climate for the German economy as a whole unexpectedly fell in January, the index for manufacturing has risen markedly, says \uc2 Joerg Kraemer\uc2  , chief economist for \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . The eight-month average in manufacturing business expectations is rising for the first time in two years and confirms the positive reading of the purchasing managers index, Kraemer says. This goes in line with \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's forecast that the decline in industrial production should end in the spring. "Nevertheless, you shouldn't get over-optimistic, as the headwinds for the German economy remain strong," Kraemer says. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects the German economy to grow 0.8% in 2020 as a whole. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1139 GMT - Although Germany's ifo business climate index fell, the good news is that business expectations among manufacturers rose for the fourth month in a row, Berenberg's economists say. The rebound in business expectations in the manufacturing sector supports the idea that the German economy will regain some momentum over the course of this year, Berenberg says. Economists \uc2 Florian Hense\uc2  and \uc2 Holger Schmieding\uc2  say the manufacturing sector has been at the core of the Germany-led downturn in the eurozone and a stabilization in its automotive sector--which continues to struggle--would be conducive to a rebound. Berenberg says German growth could return to its trend rate of 0.4% in the second half of 2020. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1139 GMT - Both the Purchasing Managers Index and the ifo survey indicate a stabilization of manufacturing activity, pointing to an end to the industrial recession in Germany, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's economist Rainer Sartoris. Although the ifo business climate showed an unexpected setback in January, there was a strong improvement in the industrial sentiment component. Signals in the service sector were mixed, with a marked improvement in the \uc2 PMI\uc2  but a decline in ifo. However, Sartoris says expectations in the sector still sit above the cyclical trough seen last year, so "one should not get too pessimistic." Despite the disappointment from ifo, "the worst days for the German economy now lie behind us," Sartoris says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1130 GMT - As the spread of the coronavirus spreads out of China fuels risk aversion, the euro could come under renewed pressure against the Japanese yen, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's Kit Juckes. "EUR/JPY shorts bearing fruit again" and if the pair falls below the 120 level, it would "look significant on charts and for sentiment," he adds. EUR/JPY fell briefly to a two month low of 119.920 and is last at 120.00, according to FactSet.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1123 GMT - Precious-metal stocks are among just a handful of FTSE 100 risers as fears about the coronavirus outbreak in China boost the price of gold and silver, as well as some safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Japanese yen. Mexican silver miner \uc2 Fresnillo\uc2  is up 0.8% and Russia and Kazakhstan-focused gold and silver producer \uc2 Polymetal International\uc2  gains 0.6%. Gold and silver prices advance 0.5% apiece to $1,580 and $18.20 an ounce respectively. "We're seeing further demand for traditional havens, such as the yen and gold, as traders seek to shield themselves from the potential economic repercussions of this virus," says Josh Mahony at trading firm IG.(philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT - The Swiss franc rises to its strongest level against the euro since April 2017 as investors turn to the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to fears about the spread of the coronavirus, with EUR/CHF hitting a low of 1.0687. Investors have further encouragement from U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Swiss franc to limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. "U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down has given the Swiss franc bulls another reason to be long," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's Kit Juckes. The U.S. added Switzerland to the Treasury's watch list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators earlier in the month as a warning to the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  . Speaking to Bloomberg at Davos, \uc2 SNB\uc2 's chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  denied the \uc2 SNB\uc2  was engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1058 GMT - January's ifo index reading shows that for the time being, the economy remains stuck at the bottom, says \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  , chief economist at ING in Germany. The first drop of the ifo index since August 2019 is "a cold shower for all optimists," Brzeski says. According to Brzeski, the decline to 95.9 shows that the German economy, particularly the manufacturing sector, has still not bottomed out. "While the service sector has already gone through a real rebound since the summer, the manufacturing sector still looks paralysed, suffering from trade conflicts and the global manufacturing downturn," Brzeski says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1034 GMT - The decline in Germany's ifo business climate index for January comes as a bit of a surprise, says Andrew Kenningham, chief Europe economist for \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . The ifo business climate index came in at 95.9 points in January, below the 97.5 forecasted by \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . "This sounds a warning note for those expecting an impressive rebound in the German growth rate," Kenningham says. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  expects the German economy to grow "at only a very anemic pace" in the first half of this year. While the manufacturing component of ifo rose, the services component fell. Kenningham says this is in line with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  ' forecast of an improvement in the export-oriented manufacturing sector coinciding with a weakening of domestic demand. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1019 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is unlikely to restart bond purchases unless it brings interest rates, currently at 0.75%, to zero, says Roberto Mialich of \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . Therefore, the BOE is unlikely to mention the possibility of quantitative easing at this Thursday's policy meeting. \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates by 25 basis points to 0.5% on Thursday and its view is that the BOE will be more aggressive in loosening monetary policy than what the market expects but "not to the point to negative interest rates," adds Mialich. The 10-year gilt yield is last down almost 4 basis points to 0.530%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT - Many market watchers neglect the risk of higher inflation, which could be a risk for bond markets in 2020, says Werner Kraemer, senior economic analyst at \uc2 Lazard Asset Management\uc2 . "Monetary expansion is entering its twelfth year, full employment prevails in many countries, wage costs are rising slightly, the state and the associated costs are expanding more and more, globalization is being increasingly restricted and the oil price is moving slowly but steadily upwards," he says. The rising gold price in recent years could also be an early indicator of inflation risks, he adds. "Therefore, it cannot be completely ruled out that the bond market, and thus the stock market, will run into difficulties again due to rising inflation rates," says Kraemer. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT - Rising sea levels are set to bruise the gross domestic product of G20 countries due to coastal flooding, even if they take ambitious action to halt climate change, says a study from the \uc2 International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis\uc2  . IIASA projects that under two scenarios--one below 2 degrees Celsius of warming by 2100 and another overshooting the target--annual global economy-wide losses could hit more than 4%. "This confirms the importance and economic efficiency of adaptation in the long term: Making sure that coastal communities and their infrastructure are climate-resilient will affect economies across the globe much less than persistent climate impacts in the absence of climate action," says study author Thomas Schinko. (dieter.holger@wsj.com; @dieterholger)\par \par \uc2 1016 GMT - Data from the \uc2 ifo Institute\uc2  shows that the mood among German companies is somewhat gloomier again, says Friederike Koehler-Geib, chief economist at KfW Group. Although the fall in the ifo business climate index came as a surprise, Koehler-Geib says there are reasons for it. The agreement between China and the U.S., as well as the orderly withdrawal of the U.K. from the EU "merely eliminate the immediate downside risks, but they don't yet create any real stimulus for the economy," Koehler-Geib says. In Germany, Koehler-Geib says an end to the industrial recession "is only dimly visible," while construction and services are showing signs of fatigue at the beginning of the year. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2020 07:11 ET (12:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200127eg1r000tg\par 