\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Coronavirus Hits Markets, Investors Seek Safety\b0\par\par\uc2 3,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2020\par 16:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Chinese coronavirus hits markets globally, causing equities, oil and base metals to fall sharply while investors flee into the perceived safety of gold, government bonds, the Swiss franc and the Japanese yen. The following is a selection of analysts' comments.\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields drop as markets remain edgy over the impact of the Chinese coronavirus. "The spreading 'coronary angst' leaves scope for the safety bid to extend," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist Rainer Guntermann. He adds that with the macro backdrop still looking inconclusive and the flow pattern improving, setbacks in Bunds should remain better buying opportunities. He sees room for eurozone government bond spreads to tighten. The week will see a load of economic data from Germany and the eurozone, starting with the Ifo business climate index on Monday. The 10-year German Bund yield is trading 1 basis point lower at -0.37%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT - Brent crude oil is down 3.4% at $58.60 a barrel and WTI futures are down 2.3% at $52.93 a barrel, with both benchmarks following broader Asian markets lower amid intensifying fears about the impact the spreading coronavirus could have on Chinese oil demand. With many Asian stock indexes closed for Chinese new year, trading is thinner than usual but those trading are "rattled by the spread of coronavirus over the weekend as China steps up efforts to contain it," Oanda's \uc2 Craig Erlam\uc2  says. Despite the Saudi energy minister's remarks about the virus's "very limited impact on global oil demand" according to Reuters, "the supply/demand dynamics are not favorable right now so it was important the energy minister stressed \uc2 OPEC\uc2  's flexibility and capability to do more," Mr. Erlam adds. (david.hodari@wsj.com; @davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT - Base-metal prices slump after Beijing extended the Lunar New Year holiday, compounding worries that the spread of coronavirus will dent demand for industrial commodities. Copper is down 1.7% on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  , falling to $5,825 a metric ton, its lowest price in almost eight weeks. Nickel and zinc both drop 2.1%, to $12,705 and $2,290 a ton respectively. "This could be a very bumpy ride," says Malcolm Freeman, director of Kingdom Futures. Still, he says the decline could represent an opportunity to buy base metals at bargain prices, and suggests doing so using long-term call options. "Past experiences suggest that the impact of viral outbreaks can be large but also short-lived," says Liam Fitzpatrick, a metals analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT - The U.K.'s FTSE 100 falls 116 points, or 1.5%, at 7470 as fears intensify over the speed at which the coronavirus is spreading. "The big risk right now for Europe and U.S. equity markets would be human-to-human [coronavirus] cases within continental Europe/North America," says \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2  of markets.com. "Without that happening the focus will be more on the risk to global growth rates given the virus is hitting the drivers of global growth." Luxury goods producer \uc2 Burberry\uc2 , which has high exposure to China, falls 4.7%. Airlines drop, with \uc2 easyJet\uc2  down 4.7% and \uc2 IAG\uc2  down 4.1%. Heavyweight mining stocks also drop sharply, with Evraz, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  and \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  all down by more than 4% while \uc2 Glencore\uc2  falls 3.9%. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT - USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2  market remains open this week, with 7.00 looking "touchable" as risk-off trade continues to dominate, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. Given China's priority of fighting the spread of the coronavirus, local governments will likely delay infrastructure projects, implying a weakening bias to economic-growth prospects in the short term, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  adds. USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2  is now up 0.7% at 6.9771, approaching both the 50- and 200-day moving averages on the \uc2 FactSet\uc2  chart. But with the stochastic oscillator way above the 80 threshold that some traders see as overbought, there is a risk that USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2  may trim its gains. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT - Gold prices rise as mounting worries about the outbreak of coronavirus hit stock markets and prompt investors to buy haven assets such as precious metals. Gold and silver futures are both up 0.4% in New York, at $1,578.10 a troy ounce and $18.18 a troy ounce respectively, after the news that the virus has infected more than 2,700 people and killed at least 80. Beijing said it was extending the annual Lunar New Year holiday by two days to Feb. 2 to delay the infection risk. As well as monitoring coronavirus, gold traders are awaiting the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's monetary-policy decision on Wednesday. There is an 87% chance that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps interest rates on hold, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2  analysis of the federal-funds futures market. (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT - Reports of the coronavirus spreading rapidly causes European stocks to fall sharply as investors spy at an excuse to sell assets that many consider to be overvalued. "Some traders were questioning the lofty valuations of stocks, so now the fear surrounding the health crisis has acted as an excuse to take money off the table," says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . The FTSE 100 is worst hit, falling 1.7% to a 6-week low as heavyweight miners, China-exposed bank \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and airlines drop sharply. Germany's DAX falls 1.5%, while France's CAC 40 loses 1.7%. The pan-European Stoxx Europe 600 index is down 1.5%, pulling away from last week's record high to hit its lowest in nearly two weeks. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT - The impact from the coronavirus on luxury stocks is transitory, with near-term risks to share prices, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  . "We would expect to see some transitory impact to consumer confidence in China as a result of [the] Coronavirus in 1H 2020 at least," says the Canadian bank, adding that Swatch, Richemont and \uc2 Burberry\uc2  are among the most exposed stocks, while \uc2 EssilorLuxottica\uc2  , Puma, Pandora and Hugo Boss remain the least exposed, according to its estimates. \uc2 RBC\uc2  says it remains positive on underlying demand conditions as actions to tackle the virus in China and abroad have been much more swift today than for SARS in 2002-2003. (mauro.orru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT - London-listed miners, travel and luxury goods stocks are all lower as fears about the spread of coronavirus in China grip markets. \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  , \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  and Antofagasta are among the mining sector's biggest fallers, with Anglo's shares dropping more than 5%, while cruise operator Carnival and \uc2 British Airways\uc2  and Iberia owner \uc2 International Consolidated Airlines Group\uc2  are the largest losers in the travel and airline industry, both down more than 4%. \uc2 Intercontinental Hotels\uc2  drops nearly 4% and Asia-focused luxury fashion retailer \uc2 Burberry\uc2  retreats almost 5%. "This has the potential to really rattle stock markets," says \uc2 Neil Wilson\uc2  at Markets.com. "With markets having been at or very near all-time highs before all this broke, this is a perfect selling opportunity." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT - European oil stocks drop as the price of Brent crude plunges more than 3% to around $58 a barrel in response to the coronavirus outbreak in China. Shares in \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  and \uc2 BP\uc2  fall 1.4% and 1.6% respectively while France's Total retreats 1.6% and Italy's \uc2 Eni\uc2  is off 1.2%. "Oil endured big losses last week and that was largely down to the health crisis in China," says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . "The country is the largest importer of oil in the world, so traders are worried the situation will curtail economic activity, and in turn demand will fall." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1038 GMT - Industrial-metal prices are sliding amid fears that the spread of coronavirus could erode demand in China, the world's largest consumer of copper and other base metals. Three-month copper futures are down 1.8% at $5,816.50 a metric ton, their lowest price since Nov. 14. Nickel prices are 2.8% lower at $12,610 a ton, their lowest price in more than six months. "We need to make sure human lives are saved and this doesn't continue, but obviously in the longer term the impact on commodities markets could be substantial," says Geordie Wilkes, head of research at brokerage \uc2 Sucden Financial\uc2  . "Workers could not be able to go back to work. In terms of refiners and smelters in China, that could reduce operating rates." (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1052 GMT - European stocks are heavily in the red as fears about the coronavirus outbreak in China spook markets. The Stoxx Europe 600 drops 1.5%, the FTSE 100 retreats 1.8% and the CAC-40 and DAX are both down 1.9%. Airlines, miners and luxury goods stocks with a strong presence in China and the wider Asian market take a particular hit. \uc2 Air France-KLM\uc2  is down 4.9% and \uc2 Deutsche Lufthansa\uc2  sheds 4.5%. \uc2 Burberry\uc2  is the biggest pan-European loser in luxury goods, off 4.8%, followed by \uc2 Christian Dior\uc2  , Hugo Boss , \uc2 LVMH Moet Hennessy Louis Vuitton\uc2  and Kering. \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  is mining's biggest faller, down 5.25%. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1059 GMT - European airline stocks fall on Monday as new coronavirus infections continue to be found outside of China, triggering concerns for the airline industry. People in the U.S., France and Australia have been diagnosed with the virus. \uc2 Air France-KLM\uc2  trades 6.7% lower at EUR8.35, while \uc2 Deutsche Lufthansa\uc2  is down 4.2% at EUR13.82. In the U.K., \uc2 easyJet\uc2  and \uc2 IAG\uc2  also trade sharply lower. (olivia.bugault@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2020 10:06 ET  Market Talk Roundup: Coronavirus Hits Markets, -2-\par \par \uc2 1105 GMT - Fund managers are slashing their investments in industrial metals amid fears that the spread of coronavirus will reduce commodity demand in China. "We're in the eye of the storm right now," says Alastair Munro, a metals salesperson at Marex Spectron. "I think we have to remain under pressure from here." Munro says investors are unwilling to take positions in metals such as copper and nickel right now even if they think prices have fallen too far, because of uncertainty about how the virus will affect supply and demand of commodities. Making investors more reluctant to bet on metals, trading is thin, with Chinese dealers away for the Lunar New Year holiday. "Liquidity's going to be key," Munro says. "Volatility will pick up". (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT - The Swiss franc rises to its strongest level against the euro since April 2017 as investors turn to the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to fears about the spread of the coronavirus, with EUR/CHF hitting a low of 1.0687. Investors have further encouragement from U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Swiss franc to limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says. "U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down has given the Swiss franc bulls another reason to be long," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's Kit Juckes. The U.S. added Switzerland to the Treasury's watch list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators earlier in the month as a warning to the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  . Speaking to Bloomberg at Davos, SNB's chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  denied the SNB was engaged in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1113 GMT - Industrial-metal prices are likely to keep falling because there is "nothing much to avert the risk sentiment in the market," says Wenyu Yao, an analyst at ING. The spread of coronavirus has prompted investors to sell copper and other metals, amid worries that the illness will disrupt industrial activity and metal imports into China. Three-month copper futures are down 1.8% at $5,818 a metric ton on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  . Aluminum prices--also dented by President Trump's decision to increase tariffs on derivative aluminum products--are down 1% at $1,765 a ton. Beijing's decision to extend the Lunar New Year holiday until Feb. 2 could "potentially hurt the demand picture in the short term," Yao says. (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1123 GMT - Precious-metal stocks are among just a handful of FTSE 100 risers as fears about the coronavirus outbreak in China boost the price of gold and silver, as well as some safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Japanese yen. Mexican silver miner \uc2 Fresnillo\uc2  is up 0.8% and Russia and Kazakhstan-focused gold and silver producer \uc2 Polymetal International\uc2  gains 0.6%. Gold and silver prices advance 0.5% apiece to $1,580 and $18.20 an ounce respectively. "We're seeing further demand for traditional havens, such as the yen and gold, as traders seek to shield themselves from the potential economic repercussions of this virus," says Josh Mahony at trading firm IG.\par \par \uc2 1130 GMT - As the spread of the coronavirus spreads out of China fuels risk aversion, the euro could come under renewed pressure against the Japanese yen, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  's Kit Juckes. "EUR/JPY shorts bearing fruit again" and if the pair falls below the 120 level, it would "look significant on charts and for sentiment," he adds. EUR/JPY fell briefly to a two month low of 119.920 and is last at 120.00, according to FactSet.(lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1156 GMT - Brent crude oil is down 3% at $58.12 a barrel and WTI futures are down 3.1% at $52.54 a barrel as fears intensify around the impact the novel coronavirus will affect Chinese oil demand. Scaling the impact of the SARS virus to 2020, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  last week forecast a drop in daily oil demand of 260,000 barrels, including a 170,000 barrel-a-day loss of jet-fuel demand. Meanwhile, Global Risk Management trader Edward Marshall says "I think we're close to peak hysteria, so yes the move is justified. We're in full panic mode." He adds, "I wouldn't be surprised to see a small bounce but until we get some hard facts were in a downtrend." (david.hodari@wsj.com; @Davidhodari)\par \par \uc2 1257 GMT - Copper prices are extending their early decline, falling to their lowest level since mid-November in London amid worries that the outbreak of coronavirus will dent consumption in China. The drop is hurting shares in major mining companies such as \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  and \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  , which are both down 4%. "The negative demand risks attached to the coronavirus are the key weight on the sector," says Nicholas Snowdon, an analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . "I think the concern is that the Chinese New Year slowdown, which always seasonally constrains demand conditions onshore through February and into early March, will be more extended as a result." Snowdon says the impact of the virus on metal demand will remain unclear for several weeks because high-frequency data from China dries up during the New Year period. (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1313 GMT - The coronavirus outbreak may have come at a particularly bad time for European hotel groups, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. "We caution that any impact on trading may be amplified, giving the timing coinciding with Chinese New Year and already weak trading in Hong Kong," \uc2 Citi\uc2  analysts say. \uc2 InterContinental Hotels Group\uc2  has a greater exposure to China than French rival Accor , with 8% of \uc2 IHG\uc2 's first-half 2019 pretax earnings before interest and around 15% of EBIT growth exposed to Greater China, whereas \uc2 Accor\uc2  's exposure is more limited, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. \uc2 IHG\uc2 's shares fall nearly 6% and \uc2 Accor\uc2  drops 4.1%. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 08:21 ET - US oil prices fall sharply early in NY as fears the coronavirus in China could hurt oil demand have quickly turned into a clear and demonstrable reduction in global oil consumption, with flights being cancelled, manufacturing plants shutting and schools closing. "The Coronavirus situation appears to be getting worse, with many traders sensing the situation may be worse than it is being reported," say analysts at \uc2 Mizuho\uc2 . "If the \uc2 WHO\uc2  reports that the situation is global, and is no longer a local phenomenon, risk assets, including Crude Oil, would most likely post another leg lower, possibly taking out 50.00 for the first time in over a year." WTI falls 3.3% at $52.43/bbl after dropping 7.5% last week. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1333 GMT - Iron-ore prices are sliding as traders worry that the outbreak of coronavirus will curtail demand for the steel ingredient in China. But Nicholas Snowdon, an analyst at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , says falling shipments of iron ore from Brazil will support prices. \uc2 Vale\uc2  is on track to export less iron ore than in any month since the height of disruption caused by the deadly collapse of a tailings dam in the Brazilian town of Brumadinho a year ago, Snowdon says. "From an iron-ore perspective, you do have significant underperformance on the supply-side dynamic which should be a relative offset to price pressures compared with some of the base metals." Iron-ore futures are down 6% at $85.34 a metric ton on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  . (joe.wallace@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1445 GMT - The global economy could take a short-term hit from the coronavirus outbreak in China, but it hasn't so far changed longer-term expectations of a slight recovery in 2020, says financial firm \uc2 Kingswood\uc2 . For global equities, news could get worse before it gets better and the market correction could continue, \uc2 Kingswood\uc2  says. "Indeed, corrections of 5-10% are surprisingly common," says \uc2 Kingswood\uc2 's Chief Investment Officer Rupert Thompson. "In the past, however, even when global health scares have impacted markets, the effect has been short-lived. If we do see a 5-10% correction, we're currently minded to use the opportunity to add to our equity holdings and move overweight from neutral. That said, we would only implement such a move following a careful reassessment." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2020 10:06 ET (15:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200127eg1r001hc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
