\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-For Swiss franc, only way to go may be up as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  steps back\b0\par\par\uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2019\par 10:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with SNB policy meeting outcome)\par \par \uc2 By Elizabeth Howcroft and John Revill\par \par \uc2 LONDON/ZURICH Dec 12 (Reuters) - A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that hurts exports and risks deflation, or a weak one that hobbles banks and annoys the U.S. -- that's the choice Switzerland faces. For now, at least, it seems to have picked the former.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc, backed by a huge balance of payments surplus, is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders love to buy in times of trouble. With a global trade war raging, the franc has gained 9% versus the euro since last April and stands less than 1% below September's two-year highs .\par \par \uc2 The gains may be signalling a shift in the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The SNB has dual mandates -- ensuring price stability and supporting the export-led economy -- that lead it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem franc gains. In 2011, it even imposed a cap on the franc's value, abandoning it only in 2015.\par \par \uc2 Now the euro-franc rate is about to fall under 1.09 for the second time in a month, a level that triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the summer.\par \par \uc2 But franc sight deposits at local banks, a proxy for measuring SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell to five-month lows of 587 billion Swiss francs ($588.65 billion) in the week ending Dec. 9, a 4.6 billion-franc net drop since October.\par \par \uc2 The SNB also refrained from following the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent interest rate cut.\par \par \uc2 So, what's changed?\par \par \uc2 Neil Mellor\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon, said the SNB's challenge "is not down to a lack of policy tools per se, but rather the likely repercussions of using them."\par \par \uc2 He was referring to the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 's criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies at its trade partners. A Treasury report in May 2019 report put Switzerland 12th on a list of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, though it removed it from a monitoring list it was put on in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has gained 2% since mid-August against the franc. Since early 2018, it's up 7%.\par \par \uc2 The next Treasury report is due in December and many reckon the SNB is holding off \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  until then. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reut.rs/35eKmYa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://reut.rs/35eKmYa}}}\par \par \uc2 "They've not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much lately," said \uc2 Marie Owens-Thomsen\uc2 , chief economist at wealth manager Indosuez. "With respect to the U.S., my feeling is that they just want to try to fly below the radar."\par \par \uc2 In September 2017, the bank tweaked its language to call the franc "significantly overvalued", versus the earlier "highly valued".\par \par \uc2 The SNB did not comment. But Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  told Swiss newspaper NZZ am Sonntag that the bank remained in close contact with the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are never intended to weaken the franc at the expense of other economies," Jordan told the paper, adding the SNB only aimed at "combating an excessive appreciation".\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the SNB left interest rates unchanged, as expected, but pledged to stay active in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to ease upward pressure on the franc.\par \par \uc2 LIMITED ABILITY\par \par \uc2 Policymakers, including Jordan, would clearly prefer a cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; in an October speech, Jordan said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bit.ly/36hJyBQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://bit.ly/36hJyBQ}}}\uc2  further franc strength would cause a "gloomy scenario", slowing the economy and increasing unemployment.\par \par \uc2 A weak franc would help the SNB to avert deflation and boost exports, which make up 65% of Switzerland's annual output. The SNB has little room to cut its benchmark interest rate; at minus 0.75% it's already the lowest in the world.\par \par \uc2 The financial sector is warning of asset bubbles and damage to savers and pension funds from deeply negative rates. A majority of the more than 2,500 company executives and entrepreneurs surveyed by \uc2 UBS\uc2  recently said they were suffering from negative interest rates on cash and savings.\par \par \uc2 As for deflation, Switzerland has seen three rounds of deflation in the past decade -- in 2009, 2012 and 2015-16. With November inflation at 0.1% year-on-year, the threat is back .\par \par \uc2 Yet \uc2 Salman Ahmed\uc2 , chief investment strategist at \uc2 Lombard Odier\uc2 , noted this was a far cry from 2015, when inflation was minus 1.5%. The economy is also growing at double the euro zone rate.\par \par \uc2 "There was a fear of widespread deflation, but inflation is near 0%, so they still have some leeway," Ahmed said.\par \par \uc2 Finally, observers note Swiss exports such as luxury watches and drugs are not especially price-sensitive. Exports rose last year to record highs despite the franc's 4% rise. And franc strength would cut companies' import costs for raw materials such as metal, cocoa and chemicals.\par \par \uc2 The franc may strengthen to 1.07 per euro in the next three months, 2% above current levels, predicts Tom Flury, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at UBS Global Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 "They can't cut rates forever, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already a bit limited given that \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  might fall into the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator pocket, which would be something which, at least from the political side, we wouldn't appreciate too much," he said.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.9972 Swiss francs)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Elizabeth Howcroft in London and John Revill in Zurich; additional reporting by Saikat Chatterjee; editing by Sujata Rao and Larry King)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-12-15T09:44:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191212efcc01ag9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
