\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT-For dollar to do well next year, nothing needs to change\b0\par\par\uc2 188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2019\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For the dollar to do well next year, nothing needs to change. The U.S. economy is beating the competition and the trade deals demanded by the United States authorities should strengthen that advantage. Interest rates in the U.S. are higher than those for other major traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been able to provide the U.S. economy with more support than the central banks have provided in other major nations, some of whom have done nothing. By contrast, a huge change is needed for the dollar to lose its advantage. That is unlikely -- interest rates would have to rise drastically. In Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering how it may do more to support the economy. The euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed quantitative easing on Nov 1 . In \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off unfavourable Swiss franc strength, and the UK is bogged down by Brexit. Chances are far better the dollar will gain advantage rather than lose it.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-11-21T11:31:27.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191118efbi01p19\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
