\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-COMMENT-The Swiss National Bank is conspicuous by its absence\b0\par\par\uc2 180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2019\par 16:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is conspicuous by its absence in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that not so long ago would have seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively trying to stem the Swiss franc's strength. Since early May, the franc has gained over 5 percent, yet sight deposits, which usually reveal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have fallen over 10 billion francs. Perhaps \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears the consequence of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, which at times in the past decade has dominated daily \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows. Switzerland converts 35% of its 768 billion francs of reserves to dollars and 9% to yen. It clearly is supporting those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in opposition of U.S. and Japanese wishes. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now during a trade war wouldn't sit well with either. Nor would an even bigger reserve that could easily surpass 1 trillion francs just to hold EUR/CHF 12% below the old 1.2000 floor. With low unemployment, little inflation and a huge trade surplus, many would say Switzerland can afford a higher franc.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-8-18T14:04:46.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020190815ef8f02cvl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
