\par\par \b \uc2 Trio of Central Banks Cut Interest Rates -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2019\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A6\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 India, New Zealand and Thailand surprise investors with aggressive reductions \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Brian Blackstone and James Glynn \f28 \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (August 8, 2019).\par \par \uc2 Three central banks in the Asia-Pacific region lowered interest rates in a surprisingly aggressive fashion Wednesday as the fallout from the U.S.-China trade war intensified, suggesting the global rate-cut cycle will gather steam in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 The trade war has hit markets hard in Southeast Asia, a fast-growing region deeply intertwined with global supply chains and dependent on trade with both the U.S. and China.\par \par \uc2 Two of the central banks that reduced rates Wednesday -- India and New Zealand -- were among the first to begin easing policy earlier this year and were a precursor to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's rate cut in late July, its first since 2008. \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced interest rates Wednesday for the first time this year, reversing a late 2018 increase. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled in July that it will consider injecting fresh stimulus when it meets in September.\par \par \uc2 With banks in the Asia-Pacific region moving again, the global easing cycle may turn out to be longer and deeper than previously expected. The Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to follow its Asian peers with another rate reduction on Thursday. Investors increased their expectations for a rate cut from \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of New Zealand's move.\par \par \uc2 "It's getting more and more difficult to make the case that central banks can fiddle around the edges on monetary policy," said Neil Shearing, chief economist at Capital Economics. "There will have to be more substantial easing."\par \par \uc2 One reason: With interest rates already ultralow or even negative in many parts of the world, central banks may need to be aggressive early on to have an effect on financial markets and exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 "There's quite limited ammunition left for many central banks; you want to use that very wisely," said Ben May, an economist at Oxford Economics. Central banks have a choice: either hold their firepower for emergencies or act early for a bigger effect, he said. The decisions out of the region Wednesday "suggest central banks are going down that second option," he said.\par \par \uc2 With central banks in big, developed countries either lowering rates or poised to do so, emerging-market central banks have greater flexibility to stimulate their economies without having to worry about capital fleeing from their financial markets to chase higher returns elsewhere. Central banks in Malaysia, Indonesia, South Korea and South Africa have also reduced policy rates in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, inflation is largely under wraps in many developing economies and well below the 2% annual growth rates that central banks in wealthy countries consider optimal. This means policy makers have little to lose by leaning on the forceful side with policy easing.\par \par \uc2 "There's not that kind of balancing act that central banks have had to do in the past," said Mr. May, referring to economic stimulus on one side and inflation fears on the other.\par \par \uc2 Most economists had expected \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make a one-quarter-percentage-point reduction. But it was double that size, to 1% from 1.5%. New Zealand "appears to be adopting the 'sooner is better' approach to monetary policy setting," said analysts at \uc2 ING Bank\uc2 , in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also surprised investors. The monetary policy committee cut its policy rate to 1.50% from 1.75%. Nine out of 11 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold steady.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key lending rate to 5.4% from 5.75%, deeper than investors expected and its lowest level in nine years as it tried to kick-start lending in Asia's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Policy easing hasn't only involved interest rates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets late last month to weaken the Swiss franc, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly sight deposit figures and monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves figures. By stepping in -- which involves purchasing foreign assets with freshly created Swiss francs -- the Swiss can weaken the franc and provide a boost to exporters, effectively easing its monetary stance.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's foreign reserves climbed by around 8 billion Swiss francs ($8.2 billion) to 768 billion francs in July, according to the SNB's monthly report Wednesday. But like many other central banks, its scope for action is limited. The SNB's foreign reserves exceed Switzerland's entire gross domestic product, raising doubts about how assertive the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be if the franc, a traditional safe haven in times of global stress, were to strengthen significantly more.\par \par \uc2 The next phase of easing for Switzerland and other central banks in Europe depends critically on the \uc2 ECB\uc2 's next steps. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  sent a strong signal last month that it was preparing a cut in its deposit rate, currently minus 0.4%, and announce a fresh round of bond purchases as soon as its next meeting on Sept. 12.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shearing said he expects a 10-basis point reduction in the \uc2 ECB\uc2 's deposit rate next month and a restart in new bond purchases -- which ended last December -- of EUR30 billion ($33.7 billion) in December.\par \par \uc2 But with German industrial production posting what Mr. Shearing called a horrific 1.5% monthly drop in June, "it's not going to take much" to move those bond buys forward, he said.\par \par \uc2 --Ben Otto and Corinne Abrams contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com and James Glynn at james.glynn@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 08, 2019 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190808ef88000qw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
