\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Head Defends Negative Rates as 'Crucial' for Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2019\par 10:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH--Swiss National Bank Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Thomas Jordan\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Friday defended negative interest rates, saying the policy is "absolutely crucial" to ensuring price stability and keeping the Swiss franc from being too strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "Unfortunately, you can't have your cake and eat it, as the saying goes," he said at the SNB's annual general meeting, explaining that "you can't have higher interest rates and a weaker Swiss franc at the same time."\par \par \uc2 He acknowledged public concerns about the more-than four-year-old policy, in particular the effect on savers who have seen their deposit rates depressed after years of ultralow rates. "Some say that it runs contrary to economic logic, and creates major problems for savers and pension funds," Mr. Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 "We would not charge negative interest if we did not regard this as being absolutely crucial for us in fulfilling our mandate," Mr. Jordan said. Without that policy, in addition to the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  would today be facing a different economic reality," with subzero inflation, weak growth and higher unemployment, he said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB has among the deepest negative policy rates in the world at minus 0.75%. It has held its deposit rate at that level since January 2015 in an effort to keep the Swiss franc from strengthening too much, which would damage exporters.\par \par \uc2 Most economists expect it to stay at this level well into next year at least. Like other European central banks outside the eurozone, the SNB is highly sensitive to what the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does. With the \uc2 ECB\uc2  expected to keep its own negative policy rate unchanged into 2020, the Swiss are likely to stand pat as well. With annual inflation in Switzerland at just 0.7%, the SNB is under no pressure to raise rates anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan's comments were made at a meeting of the SNB's shareholders. The SNB is one of the only central banks in the world with listed shares. Its share price has risen about fivefold over the past three years even though the SNB pays only a tiny dividend and the small pool of private shareholders has no say in how the bank is managed. Mr. Jordan and the other governing board members don't own any shares, according to the SNB's annual report.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the SNB reported a first-quarter profit of 30.7 billion Swiss francs ($30.1 billion), which was largely driven by valuation gains on its massive holdings of foreign stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 26, 2019 04:00 ET (08:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190426ef4q0010v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
