\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Swings to Loss -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 924 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2019\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (January 10, 2019).\par \par \uc2 ZURICH -- One year after posting a record 54 billion franc ($55 billion) profit, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  swung to a 15 billion franc loss in 2018, as a double whammy of weaker global equity markets and a stronger Swiss franc eroded the value of its massive holdings of foreign stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 The valuation loss reported Wednesday by the SNB underscores the interplay between central banks and markets. Usually, it is \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions, or hints of changes in interest rates and other policies that cause stock and bond markets to fluctuate. But this has worked in reverse for Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose finances are largely at the mercy of financial markets beyond its borders.\par \par \uc2 What sets \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  apart is that after years of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the SNB -- creating francs to purchase foreign stocks and bonds in a bid to weaken the franc -- the bank has amassed over 700 billion francs worth of foreign assets, an amount that exceeds the country's entire gross domestic product. This makes it a major asset manager in addition to being a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In terms of absolute numbers you have these high swings, but that comes from the huge size of the balance sheet and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said Alexander Koch, an economist at \uc2 Raiffeisen Schweiz\uc2  bank.\par \par \uc2 This included over $3.5 billion in \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . shares at the end of the third quarter, according to \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  filings, $2.6 billion in \uc2 Amazon Inc\uc2 . stock and $2.7 billion in \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 . The SNB's equity investments -- which comprise 20% of its foreign assets -- replicate broad indexes. The rest of the SNB's foreign-reserve portfolio is in foreign bonds. Euro assets comprise the largest share, followed by the dollar.\par \par \uc2 When equity markers rise, and when bond yields are low, the market value of the SNB's portfolio rises. A weaker franc amplifies those gains. These forces combined to push the bank's profit sharply higher in 2017. But they went in reverse in 2018.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell 4% against the franc last year, as economic and political uncertainties lifted demand for the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is typically seen as a haven in times of global stress. The dollar was little changed against the franc over 2018, but has weakened in the past month.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other asset managers, the SNB can't hedge its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, because doing so would undercut its efforts to weaken the franc.\par \par \uc2 Given that limitation, "they've done quite a good job," said Mr. Koch, and the 15 billion franc loss only represents about 2% of the bank's roughly 800 billion franc balance sheet, including gold holdings.\par \par \uc2 Other central banks like the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have amassed large portfolios consisting primarily of bonds. But those assets are denominated in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , giving them a steady source of interest income without the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 The good news for the SNB is that it can ride out market volatility, and its loss doesn't affect its ability to carry out monetary policy. Annual Swiss inflation was just 0.7% in December and with the franc still strong by historical measures, the SNB isn't under pressure to sell its foreign assets to strengthen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is expected by analysts to keep its key policy rate at minus-0.75% at least until late 2019.\par \par \uc2 The danger would come if Switzerland faced a sudden inflation shock that forced the SNB to sell its foreign assets at a loss. Repeated valuation losses could also damage the SNB's reputation at home.\par \par \uc2 Despite the 2018 loss, the SNB said it would still disperse two billion francs to the federal government and Swiss states, known as Cantons, under a profit-sharing agreement that runs through 2020. Because the SNB held on to the vast majority of its 2017 profit, it had a significant cushion to absorb last year's loss.\par \par \uc2 Its tiny pool of private investors didn't seem worried. Shares in the SNB jumped 20% Wednesday to 4,850 francs, although only around 500 shares traded hands, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Unlike most central banks, the SNB has publicly listed shares, and it pays a small, legally capped dividend of 15 francs per share to those shareholders, who have minimal ownership rights, no say in how the bank is run or how it conducts monetary policy. The SNB itself has played down the importance of its shares. For these reasons, it is hard to pinpoint why the stock rises or falls, although confirmation that the SNB would pay its dividend may have helped shares Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 According to the SNB's last annual report, it had nearly 2,200 private shareholders at the end of 2017, who held 49% of its total 100,000 shares but only about 23% of voting shares.\par \par \uc2 The bank's share price attracted global attention in 2017 and 2018 when it rose more than fourfold and approached 10,000 francs a share last April. However, it has retreated since then and despite Wednesday's gain its share price was about 50% below last year's peak.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 10, 2019 02:32 ET (07:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020190110ef1a000ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
