\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Switzerland At 'AAA/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 3,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2018\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 18 May 2018 16:11 ET *S&P Expects Switzerland's Economy Will Expand at Sound Rates in 2018-2021\par \par \uc2 18 May 2018 16:11 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Switzerland At 'AAA/A-1+'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par Ratings: \par Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 |U: AAA/Stable/A-1+ \par  \par For further details see ratings list. \par  \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We expect that Switzerland's economy will expand at sound rates in \par 2018-2021, thanks chiefly to strong exports, and supported by easing pressure \par from the Swiss franc exchange rate. \par      -- We are affirming our 'AAA/A-1+' long- and short-term ratings on \par Switzerland. \par      -- The outlook is stable. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On May 18, 2018, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its unsolicited 'AAA/A-1+' long- \par and short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on \par Switzerland. The outlook is stable. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The stable outlook on Switzerland reflects our expectation that the country's \par economy will continue to expand steadily over the next two to three years, and \par that Switzerland will maintain its institutional stability as well as its \par long-established fiscal and external buffers. \par  \par We could lower the ratings on Switzerland if policymaking became significantly \par less predictable, for example through abrupt economic policy changes with \par severe economic repercussions. Potential adverse effects of the monetary \par environment on the financial sector or the Swiss economy could also weigh on \par the ratings. \par  \par However, we currently view these scenarios as unlikely over our 2018-2021 \par outlook horizon. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par Our ratings on Switzerland reflect the country's high prosperity levels and the \par demonstrated resilience of its economy. The high competitiveness, especially of \par its exports, and consequently high current account surpluses also support the \par ratings. Furthermore, our ratings reflect Switzerland's institutional stability \par and fiscal prudence. The Swiss franc's status as an actively traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's) monetary policy credibility also support \par the ratings. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: Switzerland's economy benefits from global \par demand for its exports \par      -- We think that the Swiss economy will benefit from a favorable global \par macroeconomic environment in the near term. \par      -- We consider that persisting global demand for Swiss goods will support \par the country's export-oriented manufacturing sector and fuel investments as well \par as domestic demand in the tightening labor market and low-inflation \par environment. \par      -- Switzerland's institutions will remain an anchor of stability, in our \par view, although the Swiss franc could appreciate again if global risk \par perceptions increase, due to the country's appeal as a safe haven. \par  \par In our view, the Swiss economy is set to benefit from global economic growth, \par which drives demand for Swiss exports and in particular capital goods. At the \par same time, capacity utilization is increasing and is now slightly above its \par long-term average for the first time in seven years and, as a result, equipment \par investment is rising. The unemployment rate (national definition) fell to 2.9% \par in March from 3.4% one year earlier, which indicates rising pressure in the \par labor market on the supply side. We anticipate that higher disposable incomes \par will further underpin private consumption over our forecast horizon. The Swiss \par franc depreciated by around 10% relative to the euro over the past 12 months, \par reaching the level at which the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  had capped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until January 2015. \par This alleviates one of the main constraints on the Swiss economy, the Swiss \par franc's appreciation over the previous few years. However, we've observed--as \par in past episodes of an appreciating franc--that the Swiss economy has \par maintained its competitiveness. Swiss companies' investments in product-quality \par improvements also support our forecast of a strong export contribution to \par growth, since we now forecast the exchange rate at EUR1 to Swiss franc (CHF) \par 1.20 over the coming years. \par  \par In addition, broad-based growth is supported by the increasing population and \par continued monetary accommodation. We hence forecast real GDP growth of 2.3% in \par 2018 and 1.7% in 2019, before moderating slightly thereafter. \par  \par Switzerland's institutional effectiveness remains proverbial, and the country's \par stability, rule of law, and business environment add to its reputation as a \par beacon of stability even during crisis periods. The Swiss political system has \par a long-standing tradition of incorporating popular referenda and initiatives \par (direct democracy). While the predictability of referenda outcomes is \par inherently prone to uncertainty, Swiss policymaking has a formidable track \par record of responding to economic and societal priorities, which in part \par explains Switzerland's top positions on various business-environment ranking \par tables. \par  \par As an example of a popular initiative, in June 2018, a referendum will be held \par on whether to abolish fractional reserve banking and replace it by so-called \par sovereign money. If the referendum result favors changing the status quo, this \par would imply an untested and fundamental change to the country's monetary and \par financial system. More specifically, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be the sole creator \par of money, and commercial banks would be prohibited from creating electronic \par money, that is creating deposits through lending. All sight deposits would have \par to be backed by money created by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . We think that the \par initiative's adoption could bring high uncertainty for the banking sector, the \par role of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and potentially the economy. But as we observed with \par previous referenda, the implementation of any changes could take a long time. \par  \par Another important topic of public discussion in Switzerland is the limitation \par of immigration, and new referenda on the free movement of people could be on \par the agenda toward the end of our outlook horizon. These discussions touch on \par the six bilateral agreements that are connected to the agreement on the free \par movement of people within the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Ease of EU  market access is of crucial \par importance for Swiss companies and, as such, we have observed that Switzerland \par has adapted legislation to comply with the bilateral agreements. Switzerland is \par currently negotiating a framework agreement with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   to codify how \par Switzerland will adapt its own legislation to changes in EU  law. We note that \par Switzerland's relations with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   will remain a subject of national \par discourse, and we do not believe there will be any disruptions in Switzerland's \par relationship with the EU . \par  \par Flexibility and Performance Profile: Monetary policy remains accommodative and \par conscious of exchange rate developments, while fiscal and external positions \par are solid \par      -- We project that Switzerland will post general government surpluses in \par 2018-2021, reflecting the country's prudent fiscal policy. \par      -- We expect Switzerland's current account surplus will remain at a high \par 7% of GDP on average in 2018-2021, bolstered by export strength. \par      -- While we believe inflation will remain well below 2% over the next few \par years, we anticipate that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , if \par needed, to ease \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pressures. \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's central government posted a sizable surplus of around 0.5% of GDP \par in 2017, which would have been even higher if the government had not made a \par provision for tax refunds it has to pay. Currently, tax payers choose to leave \par the money with the state to avoid negative interest rates on deposits. The \par fiscal results have been outperforming the budgets in the past years, and we \par expect general government surpluses will persist over the coming years. This is \par because of continued tax revenue growth, and previous years' savings measures \par and expenditure restraint, enacted even in times of solid fiscal performance. \par We expect fiscal prudence will continue, to comply with the Swiss debt brake \par rule and also to prepare for some potential fiscal pressures. For example, the \par reform of corporate taxation aims to abolish several corporate tax privileges \par that would no longer be internationally accepted, while retaining competitive \par tax rates. The reform, which is currently in the legislative process, could \par have an adverse effect on tax revenues and also trigger shifts in the Swiss \par intercantonal equalization system from 2020. We estimate that the potential \par impact on the federal government would amount to roughly CHF1 billion per year \par (0.1% of 2018 GDP). In that regard, we note that Swiss public finances are \par highly decentralized. Cantons and municipalities account for about one-half of \par total general government revenues and spending; however, we consider that the \par impact would be limited. \par  \par As a result of a solid budgetary performance and economic growth, we expect net \par general government debt to decrease to about 16% of GDP by 2021 from 20% in \par 2017 as net issuance reduces further in the coming years. \par  \par One of the key pillars of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary policy, in addition to negative \par interest rates, has been its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. \par These measures intended to stem the strong appreciation of the Swiss franc \par following the decision to abandon the franc's cap against the euro in 2015. \par Although the Swiss franc had appreciated in 2007-2017 by about 20% in real \par terms, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently depreciated vis-a-vis the euro, trading at around \par the level at which it was fixed before 2015. This has eased some of the \par economic pressures on corporate margins, labor demand, and the tourism sector. \par As a result of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves increased to \par CHF738 billion (about $738 billion) in March 2018, from CHF522 billion in April \par 2014. Due to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's balance-sheet expansion, it has become more exposed than \par any major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  globally to valuation shifts. Consequently, the recent \par trend of an appreciating euro against the franc has contributed to the further \par accounting-based increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, and we understand that \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets since last year. We \par do not expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  to pre-empt any efforts by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   to \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 18 May 2018 16:11 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Switzerland At -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par normalize monetary policy. Because the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  is holding large amounts of eurozone \par government bonds and U.S. equities, any steps toward normalization would likely \par be carefully weighed regarding their potential market effects. Negative \par interest rates have had unintended consequences, including rich valuations in \par the property sector. Despite the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's efforts to tackle low inflation, \par consumer prices rose only slightly in 2017, following five years of deflation, \par and we expect inflation to climb gradually to 1.3% by 2021. This expectation \par could change, however, if the franc appreciates again, for example if external \par risks lead to safe-haven inflows into the franc and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and negative rates did not suffice to counteract those potential \par pressures. \par  \par We expect that Switzerland will continue to post a sizable and stable current \par account surplus of about 7% of GDP on average throughout our forecast horizon, \par owing to strong expected export performance. We also project the country will \par remain a net external creditor, well in excess of 100% of current account \par receipts (CARs) regardless of potential valuation effects on Switzerland's \par international investment position. By a narrower measure of external debt, net \par of public- and financial-sector external assets, we expect Switzerland will \par remain in a debtor position, averaging 14% of CARs during our forecast horizon. \par We view the franc as an actively traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It holds almost a 5% share in \par spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions, according to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International \par Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2  ' Triennial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Survey in 2016. \par  \par Switzerland has a prominent financial sector with total assets representing \par almost 5x GDP, mainly from the two largest Swiss banks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS AG\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit \par Suisse AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  . Domestic assets denominated in franc are about 3x GDP. Since the \par financial crisis and substantial government support for \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the government has \par taken regulatory measures to reduce systemic risk and improve the resolvability \par of systemic banks, which should help contain future contingent liabilities for \par the sovereign. \par  \par We think that growth in residential real estate prices and mortgage lending \par over the past several years could pose a risk, especially for Swiss banks \par focusing on the domestic market and with large exposures to the Swiss real \par estate market. We think that mortgage lending, which represents 85% of domestic \par bank lending, will increase by 2%-3% in 2018, although recent growth rates are \par considerably lower than in 2012-2015. We think that ongoing efforts by the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Swiss regulator to contain mortgage debt, using \par macroprudential and self-regulatory measures, will constrain the growth of \par imbalances in this market. Another potential risk generally stems from the need \par to adapt to the negative-interest-rate environment. Under our Banking Industry \par Country Risk Assessment, we classify Switzerland in group '2' (on a scale of \par '1' to '10', with group '1' denoting the lowest-risk banking systems), one of \par the strongest worldwide. We currently view the government's contingent \par liabilities from its financial sector and government-related entities as \par limited. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 18,2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings - April 07,2017 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History - May 7, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - April 10, 2018 \par      -- 2016 Sovereign Ratings Update: Outlook And CreditWatch Resolutions - \par April 18, 2017 \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends: First-Quarter 2018 - April 11, 2018 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2017 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions - May 08, 2017 \par      -- Banking Industry Country Risk Assessment Update: November 2017, Nov. \par 10, 2017 \par