\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Swiss play down inclusion on U.S.  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulation list\b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2017\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, comment, latest data on sight deposits at SNB)\par \par \uc2 By John Revill\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, April 18 (Reuters) - The Swiss government on Tuesday played down Switzerland's reappearance on a \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  watch list of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, saying the status would have no immediate consequences.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , according to its legal mandate, follows its own independent monetary policy," the Swiss finance ministry said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "Their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases are not aimed at devaluing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raising the competitiveness of Swiss exports," it added. "Its goal is to limit the extent of the franc's overvaluation and prevent a strong downward push in inflation."\par \par \uc2 Switzerland, which was also mentioned by the Treasury last October, appeared along with China, Japan, South Korea, Taiwan and Germany on the latest list which aims to curb what the U.S. calls "unfair \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices".\par \par \uc2 The Swiss qualified because for years they have used \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases to counter persistent inflows into the safe-haven franc. The SNB has also introduced negative interest rates to make the franc less attractive.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped by nearly 15 billion Swiss francs ($14.93 billion) in March to 683.18 billion francs, larger than Swiss gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Sight deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a proxy for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , showed the SNB continued to sell francs ahead of the French presidential elections starting on Sunday, according to the latest data on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The SNB was not immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 Instead of market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  should rely more on fiscal policy and interest rates to support its economy and ward off demand for the franc, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said in its report on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland met two of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation criteria -- having a material current account surplus at 10.7 percent of GDP in 2016 -- and having engaged in a "sizeable one-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases", the U.S. report said.\par \par \uc2 At $13.7 billion Switzerland did not however have enough of a bilateral trade surplus with the United States to meet the third criteria, which could trigger bilateral talks aimed at correcting the situation.\par \par \uc2 "As long as Switzerland is only on the watch list, it isn\u8217\'20\'19t a problem," said David Marmet, an economist at \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "Switzerland fulfils two of the three criteria, but it cannot be long before the trade balance means it meets all three, when it could become a problem.\par \par \uc2 "Still the Swiss will try to explain to the Americans their situation," Marmet said. "I think the SNB would prefer not to be on the list, but I don't think it will affect its monetary policy \u8211\'20\'13 what else can they do?" (Additional reporting by Angelika Gruber, Editing by Michael Shields) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-18T10:01:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170418ed4i00bg1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
