\par\par \b \uc2 Why Are Europe's Small Central Banks Stocking Up Foreign Money?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 1,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 14:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ZURICH--Europe's smaller central banks are loading up on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at rates usually associated with periods of intense global stress, highlighting the fragile underpinnings of the global economic recovery despite the recent upbeat mood in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's holdings of foreign assets jumped last month at their fastest pace in over two years as its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fought the strong franc, which weakens exports and inflation. The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January on a massive scale to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  target against the euro. Denmark has also stepped up its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases to keep the krone from strengthening too much.\par \par \uc2 These central banks are showing crisis-like behavior to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in the absence of obvious trouble. This exposes them to losses if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fail to weaken on their own. It also raises doubts as to how long they can keep this up in an era when economic and political uncertainties appear to be a lasting feature of the world economy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a little bit of survivor behavior," said Peter Rosenstreich, head of market strategy at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 . "They've been protecting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so long and it's hard to give up that defensive position."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said Tuesday its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves swelled nearly 25 billion Swiss francs ($24.63 billion) last month to 668 billion francs, the biggest rise since December 2014, the month before the Swiss abandoned a cap on the franc's value. The pile of foreign reserves is greater than Switzerland's entire gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite bizarre. You'd think at some time you'd run out of surprises, " said Stefan Gerlach, chief economist at BSI Bank in Zurich and a former deputy governor at \uc2 Ireland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The franc is regarded as one of the world's safest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  given Switzerland's stable economy and low debt, meaning its value usually increases when investors seek safety instead of returns.\par \par \uc2 Analysts cited elections in France this spring, in Germany this fall and continued political uncertainty in Italy, the U.S. and U.K. as reasons for the relentless demand for the franc and other safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Central banks accumulate foreign reserves when they purchase assets denominated in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , using freshly created money. They do this to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , protecting exports and giving a boost to inflation. Foreign reserves can waver slightly due to changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values, but big increases like \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's signal aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This tool has gained traction in recent years as official rates have turned negative in Denmark and Switzerland and are near zero in the Czech Republic.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 14.5 billion euros ($15.3 billion) in January, the largest amount by far since it introduced an exchange rate target against the euro in November 2013. "It is an eye-popping number," said Bruna Skarica, economist at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 Czech officials have said they plan to discontinue this target in the middle of this year, which has spurred investor flows into the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the koruna, in anticipation.\par \par \uc2 Denmark \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to the tune of 4.7 billion kroner ($670 million), the most since last June.\par \par \uc2 Like the franc, the Danish krone typically rises in uncertain times, especially in the eurozone. The Czech Republic is seen as one of the more stable economies within central and Eastern Europe, so it sees similar trends.\par \par \uc2 What sets this latest episode apart is that there wasn't a particular destabilizing stress point in the last few weeks like the Greek crisis or the U.K. vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Global markets have been mostly calm with U.S. equities marking record highs. Government bond yields have risen but not too much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are close to hitting their 2% inflation targets, and the Fed is expected to raise interest rates as soon as next week.\par \par \uc2 This should be a recipe for reduced demand for the Swiss, Danish and Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors seek higher returns elsewhere. But the opposite has occurred.\par \par \uc2 The euro traded at 1.0723 francs midday Tuesday, about 10% lower than it was when the SNB had the franc ceiling in place from 2011 to early 2015. The euro fetched 7.43 Danish kroner, which is within the Danish bank's target of within 2.25% above or below 7.46. The Czech koruna is near the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 27 against the euro, but only due to the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The global economy is doing better than in the last few years" and has broadened out, SNB Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  said in a Swiss newspaper interview on March 4.\par \par \uc2 But alluding to the contrast between a better economy and heightened political tension that has bedeviled his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Jordan noted the political risks are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 "Such phases of increased political uncertainty are problematic for us because the Swiss franc is considered a safe haven," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This article was corrected at 8:56 a.m. ET because the original version misstated the amount of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 11th paragraph. The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 14.5 billion euros ($15.3 billion) in January, not 14.8 billion euros as originally stated.\par \par \uc2 7 Mar 2017 09:10 ET  Correction to Europe's Small Central Banks Story\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 14.5 billion euros ($15.3 billion) in January, the largest amount by far since it introduced an exchange rate target against the euro in November 2013. ("Why Are Europe's Small Central Banks Stocking Up Foreign Money?," at 1313 GMT, misstated the amount of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 11th paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 07, 2017 09:10 ET (14:10 GMT)\pa