\par\par \b \uc2 Europe Political Risks Boost Swiss Franc, Support Gold -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:52 ET - For a good measure of European political uncertainty, look no further than the Swiss franc. The safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up around 3% against the euro since September, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at limiting the franc's appreciation. In 2017, it has risen around 0.7% as populists loom large in Dutch elections this month and French elections are set for April, says \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  . Meanwhile, gold, another gauge of market uncertainty, is up around 7% this year despite a dollar rally in February. The euro was recently unchanged at 1.0641 against the Swiss franc. Gold was down 1% at $1,237.50 a troy ounce. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:40 ET - \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  ' (DAL ) February unit revenues were flat, compared with investor expectations according to Cowan, which downgraded its 1Q EPS forecast to 83 cents a share, down from 94 cents. "We believe the company is tracking to the low end of their expected guidance range of flat to up 2%," it said. The disappointing results aren't a function of demand, Cowan adds, but "rather due to continued choppiness of yields." DAL shares were down 1.7% Thursday to $50.16. (susan.carey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:32 ET - And we're off! SNAP has begun trading... its at 24.70, an immediate 45% pop for the IPO investors who paid $17 last night. SNAP's opening price of $24 gave the company a market valuation of $33.3B on a diluted basis. (chris.dieterich@wsj.com and erik.holm@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:28 ET - SNAP CEO Evan Spiegel took home more cash in the year leading up to the company's IPO--without accounting for inflation--than the CEOs of \uc2 Match Group\uc2  (MTCH), \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB), \uc2 Pandora Media\uc2  (P), ETSY, \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (TWTR) and Square (SQ), ahead of all of those companies' public offering, a new study says. In 2016, Spiegel's salary from SNAP was $503,205, the study conducted by \uc2 Equilar\uc2  says. CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  received $483,333 from FB in 2011, the study says. Spiegel also received the largest CEO bonus, of $1M, of any of these CEOs in the years before their companies went public, according to the study. SNAP soars as shares trade for the first time, up 44% to $24.41. (georgia.wells@wsj.com; @georgia_wells)\par \par \uc2 11:14 ET - In the job market for new college graduates, a degree held by a student's parents or grandparents can go a long way, according to a report from the National \uc2 Association of Colleges and Employers\uc2  . In a survey of over 5,000 graduating college seniors, just 43% of first-generation college students had a job offer in hand by graduation, compared with 49% of non-first generation students. The Association points to money as a major factor influencing job-placement before graduation--first-generation students accepted only 58% of the offers they received, often citing insufficient starting salaries, compared with 69% acceptance by non-first generation students. (kelsey.gee@wsj.com; @kelseykgee)\par \par \uc2 11:10 ET - Car makers relied heavily on discounts in February to prop up monthly sales tallies with every mass-market manufacturer spending more on incentives year-over-year, according to RBC. While total US auto sales were down 1% last month, industry-wide spending increased 18% over the same period a year ago, it says, citing figures from \uc2 Autodata Corp\uc2  . GM led the increase with a 27.4% jump in promotional spending, thanks in part to aggressive deals during its February "Truck Month" event. The Detroit giant, however, argues its internal numbers show incentives over average transaction prices were flat. \uc2 Ford\uc2  's (F) spending increased 20.9%, \uc2 Fiat Chrysler\uc2  's (FCAU) was up 10.6%, \uc2 Nissan\uc2  's (NSANY)incentives were up 20.1% and \uc2 Honda\uc2  (HMC) was up 26.6% YOY. (christina.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:04 ET - Snap hasn't organized any company parties to celebrate the company's public offering for workers in San Francisco or Los Angeles, employees say. Instead, some employees watched the opening bell from home and individual departments have made dinner reservations. Snap has not made any company-wide warnings to employees about flashy celebrations of new wealth, an employee says, despite a small protest outside the company's offices over rising rents in the artsy beach town of Venice where Snap has its largest offices. (Georgia.wells@wsj.com; @georgia_wells)\par \par \uc2 11:00 ET - The current reimbursement system for prescription drugs--with rebates negotiated by middlemen--works "reasonably well" to control spending for employers and health insurers, but it's not great for patients. That's the view of Cowen analysts in a new report deconstructing drug reimbursement. "What is not working, however, is the burden to the patient caught in the middle, as benefit design changes have exposed her to rising first-dollar cost through high-deductible plans and co-insurance tied to rising list prices," they write. Cowen suggests more point-of-sale rebate use for patients and inflation caps to mitigate the cost burden, as well as value-based contracts that tie reimbursement to how well a drug works in patients. \uc2 Cigna\uc2  (CI) has signed several such deals in recent years with drug manufacturers. (peter.loftus@wsj.com; @Loftus)\par \par \uc2 10:54 ET - Bills, or Treasury debt that mature in a year or less, are the hardest hit today. The yield on the benchmark 3-month bill jumped five basis point to 0.683%, a large move for this section of the bond market. The 6-month bill yield is also 5bps higher at 0.837%. "The bond market is giving the boy who cried wolf credibility to the Fed," said Jason Evans, co-founder of hedge fund NineAlpha Capital. "The bar is very low for the Fed to raise rates in March" unless the jobs report--due March 10--"is shockingly anemic." Fed funds futures showed the odds of a rate increase by the mid-March meeting jumped to 75% Thursday, up from 27% at the end of last week, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2  . (min.zeng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:52 ET - Traders say the state of financial markets would allow the Fed to hike in as soon as two weeks. The risk, though, is if the Fed stands pat because it could make it more difficult to guide market expectations in the future. "Should equity and risk markets continue to behave, it's likely that the Fed would feel comfortable enough from a financial conditions perspective to pull the trigger in March," says Ian Lyngen, head of US rates strategy at \uc2 BMO\uc2  . The risk to the Fed strategy, he says, is that if the Fed keeps pushing such hike probabilities closer to 100% before the meeting, "jaw boning may then be less effective in the future if the Fed subsequently is forced to pass in March." (min.zeng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:42 ET - The Materials Price Index, tracked by \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  , fell for the first time in 17 weeks last week, breaking the longest winning streak on record. The index tracks commodities including crude oil, metals, fibers, lumber and others, and leading the decline last week were chemicals and rubber. While commodities have gotten a boost this year from bets on global growth and inflation, prices ran into headwinds last week such as a stronger US dollar on the belief the Fed is likely to raise rates in the coming months. A decline in US manufacturing data in February also put a damper on enthusiasm for materials, \uc2 IHS\uc2  said. Meanwhile, a slowdown in China's property sector could put more pressure on commodities if it continues. (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1542 GMT - German discount chain \uc2 Aldi\uc2  will push into China starting March 20, launching on Tmall with wines, snacking and breakfast products. The company's plan to enter China digitally marks a first, with \uc2 Aldi\uc2  mainly selling through brick and mortar stores elsewhere in the world. \uc2 Aldi\uc2  's entry, while limited for now, poses a further threat to rival \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  on whose turf it's already stomping in the U.S. Aldi will supply China using Australian suppliers. (Saabira.Chaudhuri@wsj.com , @SaabiraC)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 02, 2017 11:52 ET (16:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170302ed32002of\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
