\par\par \b \uc2 Switzerland's U.S. Exports at Record High -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone \par \uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2017\par 17:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ZURICH--Switzerland's exports to the U.S. surged last year to a record high, pushing the trade surplus higher and putting the Alpine export powerhouse in a potentially uncomfortable position amid rising protectionist sentiment in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The figures come alongside heightened attention brought by President Donald Trump to bilateral trade balances and the policies countries have pursued to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar to gain a competitive edge.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland has largely escaped much focus in the U.S. and is unlikely to be in the new administration's crosshairs now, given its relatively small size.\par \par \uc2 Still, its swelling surplus, and the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's multiyear efforts to weaken the franc, could at a minimum raise questions as to why the U.S. may treat some countries like China and Mexico more harshly than others down the road when it comes to trade.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's overall trade surplus was 37.5 billion Swiss francs ($37.6 billion) last year, the country's customs office said Thursday, up one billion francs from 2015 and an all-time high. Nearly half of that surplus--17.2 billion francs--came from the U.S., as Swiss exports there jumped 15% to 31.5 billion francs.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland ran smaller trade surpluses with Japan and the Middle East, while it had trade deficits with Germany and China.\par \par \uc2 "Looking forward if this is truly Donald Trump's agenda to level the playing field, Switzerland has to be on that list," said Peter Rosenstreich, head of market strategy at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China, not Switzerland, has been a center of attention for the new U.S. administration. When he was a candidate, Mr. Trump said he would label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on the first day of his administration, though he hasn't done so yet.\par \par \uc2 Yet the latest trade figures put Switzerland closer to China in satisfying all three criteria the Treasury Department has used to monitor the actions of U.S. trading partners: a significant trade surplus with the U.S. which the Treasury defines as over $20 billion; large current account surplus; and active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account surplus was 9% of gross domestic product in 2016, according to \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  estimates, well above the 3%-of-GDP level the Treasury considers material.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has in recent years engaged in one-way \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc, thereby making Swiss exports more competitive in world markets. The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has for years said the franc was significantly overvalued.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  investments--which are made by creating francs and buying foreign bonds and stocks--have swelled from under 50 billion francs 10 years ago to nearly 670 billion francs in the third quarter of 2016.\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 0.9973 against the franc midday Thursday, down slightly from Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "If [declaring a country a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator] is supposed to be supported by facts, then Switzerland is a bigger enemy," said Marie Owens Thomsen, chief economist at Indosuez Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 In October, the Treasury Department added Switzerland to its "monitoring list" of countries that have met two of its three criteria, joining China, Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Germany.\par \par \uc2 Yet Switzerland is unlikely to raise the ire of the U.S. soon. Its surplus, though quite large relative to Switzerland's small economy, pales in comparison to China's $319 billion surplus with the U.S. through November last year or the \uc2 EU\uc2 's EUR105 billion ($113 billion) surplus through November.\par \par \uc2 And most of Switzerland's export growth to the U.S. and elsewhere has come in chemicals and pharmaceuticals, high-value industries that don't necessarily cost the U.S. a lot of manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 "The underlying story is really a different one if you look at Switzerland's trade surplus with the U.S. versus the Chinese one," said Ronald Indergand, head of short-term economic analysis at Switzerland's economic affairs department.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the U.S. has recognized that Switzerland is in a tough spot with limited room for maneuver of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight falling consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 But if the U.S. starts handing out the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator tag, and bases it on the criteria the Treasury Department has used in the past, Switzerland may not be able to avoid attention for long.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump "may be ticking off a protectionist trade war with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation one of motivating factors, but if he does that where does it stop?" said Mr. Rosenstreich.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 26, 2017 11:02 ET (16:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170126ed1q0038e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
