\par\par \b \uc2 Central Banks Still  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Markets Despite Weakened Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone   \par \uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2016\par 16:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH--Central banks in parts of Europe and South America have responded to the aftershocks of Brexit by resorting to a tried-and-true tool: \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Data Thursday from the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscore the lengths officials have gone to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much at a time most countries seem to prefer weaker, not stronger, exchange rates. Its foreign reserves rose to 95% of the size of the Swiss economy last month, exposing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to huge losses if the franc keeps strengthening.\par \par \uc2 The effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has waned, however, as negative interest rates and large-scale bond instruments emerged as the weapons of choice to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and give economies a boost through higher exports and more inflation.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls into the category of doing something, without really doing anything," said Peter Rosenstreich, chief market analyst at Swissquote Bank in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 The policy's usefulness has been debated for decades. When a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets--sometimes at the direction of its finance ministry--it typically uses newly created money to buy its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it considers it to be too weak or sell it to buy other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  announced its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 24, hours after the U.K. decision to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  was announced. Its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increased last month to 608 billion Swiss francs ($624 billion), up 6 billion Swiss francs from the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Despite the efforts to weaken the franc, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen 2% against the euro and 10% against the pound in the last month while holding mostly steady against the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has effects but it takes a massive amount especially in an open economy such as Switzerland," said \uc2 Joseph Gagnon\uc2 , senior fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold Danish kroner to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep the euro within a mandated target range. Analysts at \uc2 Nordea\uc2  said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaled 25.2 billion kroner ($3.8 billion) last month, pushing total \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to 452 billion kroner, nearly 25% of gross domestic product. "That's really a lot," said Jan Storup Nielsen, chief analyst at Nordea. "It requires more on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  side to get the same result."\par \par \uc2 It isn't just developed economies deploying these tactics. \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in on July 1 to try to slow the real's gains against the dollar after it hit an 11-month high. It did this by auctioning off reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps contracts, a derivative contract that has the effect of supporting the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Switzerland and Denmark, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise wasn't due to safe-haven flows but rather higher interest rates, which tend to raise global demand for a country's assets.\par \par \uc2 "Markets reacted to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's signal, but that won't last long, " said Pedro Paulo Silveira, chief economist at the Nova Futura brokerage in S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo. "The interest rate differential will continue to push the real up against the dollar."\par \par \uc2 Ideally, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a double-barreled effect. Buying or selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself affects the price. And, symbolically, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unlimited money-printing powers send a powerful signal to investors that they risk heavy losses by betting against it.\par \par \uc2 This is magnified when countries join forces. In the mid-1980s, major economies coordinated actions to weaken the dollar and strengthen the yen at a time of rising protectionist sentiment in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, central banks from the world's largest economies bought euros to support the eurozone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was still in its infancy. Economists are divided on how much of an effect this had, but the euro rose steadily in the decade after the move.\par \par \uc2 That era of coordinated action largely ended and for the last 15 years has been limited to unilateral action by Japan, Switzerland and others.\par \par \uc2 "I remain pretty skeptical of this idea that discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can really do very much," said Stephen Cecchetti, former chief economist at the Bank for International Settlements. "There is some evidence...that you could damp things down a bit" by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 Economists say that the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been weakened as central bankers broadened the types of assets they purchased. In recent years, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  have bought trillions of dollars, euros and yen in government and other bonds to increase the money supply and reduce long-term interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Many central banks have also made long-term loans available to financial institutions at superlow rates.\par \par \uc2 With these vast sums sloshing around the markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by smaller economies may prove futile beyond keeping things stable for a little while.\par \par \uc2 And exchange rate-dependent \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have actually hastened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 's demise. In January 2015, the SNB abandoned without warning a floor on the euro's value against the franc that it had defended for more than three years. The move stunned financial markets and led to a 23 billion franc loss by the SNB last year.\par \par \uc2 It also weakened one of the most important elements of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , credibility, particularly as the U-turn came from one of the world's most conservative central banks.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB giving up the floor was the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an effective policy tool," said Mr. Rosenstreich.\par \par \uc2 -- Jeffrey Lewis in Sao Paulo and Charles Duxbury in Stockholm contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 07, 2016 10:57 ET (14:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160707ec770027m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
