\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  ramps up  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\uc2  after Brexit\b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2016\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, July 4 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  (SNB) went on its biggest foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying spree since January 2015 in the wake of Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , data showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Commercial and other deposits with the SNB rose to 507.514 billion Swiss francs ($520.69 billion) from 501.231 billion the previous week, indicating the bank had bought \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market and then credited depositors' accounts.\par \par \uc2 The bank is using negative rates, coupled with an unspecified amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases, to weaken the franc and protect exports to the euro zone, Switzerland's biggest trading partner.\par \par \uc2 "Brexit has triggered a big demand for safe havens like the franc, so the SNB has to keep acting," J. Safra Sarasin \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ursina Kubli said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave rare confirmation it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt appreciation of the franc within hours of Britain's referendum becoming clear on June 24.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that its unusual step of making its intentions clear was intended to maximise the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SNB declined to comment on its future strategy to limit the franc's strength.\par \par \uc2 Swiss manufacturers have struggled with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength which reduces their profitability.\par \par \uc2 "If the franc went back to 1.10 versus the euro, it would be a significant relief," \uc2 Hans Hess\uc2 , president of manufacturers' lobby \uc2 Swissmem\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 On Monday the franc traded at about 1.08 per euro. ($1 = 0.9747 Swiss francs) (Reporting by John Revill; Additional reporting by Joshua Franklin; Editing by Louise Ireland)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-7T10:00:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160704ec7400439\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
