\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  confirms  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  after Brexit\b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 12:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday gave rare confirmation that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the Swiss franc in the wake of Britain's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the back of the unexpected Leave vote, the safe-haven franc rose <EURCHF=EBS> to its highest level against the euro since August 2015 and had its biggest one-day jump since the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  removed its franc peg to the euro on Jan. 15, 2015.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of the SNB's actions, the euro steadily rose from a low of 1.0623 to around 1.0790 francs in early London trade.\par \par \uc2 "Following the United Kingdom's vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the Swiss franc came under upward pressure," the SNB said in a statement. "The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize the situation and will remain active in that market."\par \par \uc2 The SNB normally declines to comment on whether or not it has been active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but has occasionally given confirmation in the past, most recently in June last year amid uncertainty over Greece's financial future.\par \par \uc2 Last week, SNB officials said it would counter any surge in an already overvalued franc should Britain vote to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2 , leaving open the option to cut record-low rates deeper into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 Global financial markets plunged as complete results showed a near 52-48 percent split for leaving, sparking fears the decision will hit investment in the world's fifth-largest economy, threaten London's role as a global financial capital and foment uncertainty in the world's biggest trading bloc.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Joshua Franklin; Editing by Michael Shields) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-24T10:08:37.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160624ec6o0161y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
