\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  chief warns of limits of money policy\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2016\par 17:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  chief warns of risks from loose money\par \par \uc2 * Jordan says money policy alone will not solve problems\par \par \uc2 * Franc strengthens on remarks\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official plays down any rate cut (Adds comments about negative rates)\par \par \uc2 By Michael Shields and John O'Donnell\par \par \uc2 ZURICH/ FRANKFURT, Feb 23 (Reuters) - Loose monetary policy may be reaching its limits, the head of the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled on Tuesday, as he cautioned that it alone would not be enough to shield countries from global economic problems.\par \par \uc2 Some see the comments, delivered by \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  in Frankfurt, as a hint to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which oversees the 19-member euro zone, that it should carefully weigh any further loosening.\par \par \uc2 Equally, however, they were taken by some investors as a signal that Switzerland's appetite for further action of its own was fading.\par \par \uc2 "The options are not unlimited," Jordan told an audience of students and academics. "The effects of monetary policy measures can wane with duration and dosage."\par \par \uc2 Jordan also echoed Sweden in playing down the usefulness of higher penalties on lenders by charging "negative rates" on those who hoard money with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Interest rates ... cannot continue to be lowered into negative territory without at some point precipitating a flight to cash," he said, although he added later: "The fear that the introduction of negative interest will precipitate a flight to cash has thus far proved unfounded."\par \par \uc2 Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Martin Floden had said that cutting negative interest rates further would not have much effect, supporting views that the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  may hold off on further policy loosening.\par \par \uc2 Commenting on action by central banks around the globe, Jordan cautioned of the "potential cost" of such loose money policy while adding: "Monetary policy cannot cushion the impact of every negative development in the global economy or the international financial markets."\par \par \uc2 His frank comments illustrate that, after years of rock bottom borrowing costs and vast money printing around the globe, there is a growing feeling among central bankers that they may have spent much of their ammunition.\par \par \uc2 The roll-out of a 1.5-trillion-euro money printing programme in the euro zone helped depress the euro and strengthen the franc, requiring \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  to embark on costly \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the franc becoming too strong.\par \par \uc2 Monetary policy alone will not solve the problem, said Jordan, adding, however, that he remains willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed.\par \par \uc2 Jordan said the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's policy of negative interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was working although this too had limits.\par \par \uc2 "The new (Swiss) monetary policy stance is beginning to have an impact," he said, noting the franc had actually weakened over the past 12 months. "Nonetheless, the Swiss franc remains considerably overvalued against the euro in real terms."\par \par \uc2 In January 2015, Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocked financial markets by abandoning a cap of 1.20 francs per euro it had defended for more than three years to shield the export-oriented economy from the pain of an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro plunged against the franc when the SNB cap was removed but has recovered this year to over 1.11 francs and was trading around 1.0925 at 1610 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and quantitative easing programmes carry with them the increasing risk that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to conduct monetary policy may be compromised in the long term," Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 Jordan said earlier this month the SNB could push interest rates deeper into negative territory, warning that turmoil in Europe could revive the franc's traditional role as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Additional reporting by Simon Johnson in Stockholm; Editing by Brenna Hughes Neghaiwi and Alison Williams)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-26T16:53:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160223ec2n016o5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
