\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sees low risk of downward wage, price spiral\b0\par\par\uc2 308 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2015\par 01:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA, Oct 10 (Reuters) - Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees a low risk of a negative price and wage spiral and is hopeful that a recent "slight" depreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue, the head of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on the sidelines of \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  meetings in Lima, SNB Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  repeated a pledge to keep interest rates in negative territory and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrestling with falling consumer prices, weak economic growth and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the SNB in January abruptly abandoned its cap of 1.20 francs per euro. The franc is now trading around 1.09 per euro.\par \par \uc2 "We saw a certain depreciation of the franc but the Swiss franc remains overvalued, significantly overvalued, and of course this creates a difficult situation for the Swiss economy," Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 "We have negative interest rates and we have also the readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary, and both should contribute to a further weakening of the Swiss franc."\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices fell 1.4 percent in the year through September but Jordan said the chance of a negative downward spiral in wages and prices was limited.\par \par \uc2 "This risk is limited because we have at the moment...oil prices and we have the exchange rate shock, and if the exchange rate does not further appreciate and oil prices do not fall further, we should have a stabilization in the inflation outlook and then go back to zero inflation in the beginning of 2017."\par \par \uc2 Jordan declined to say whether the SNB had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since its last foray into the markets in late June. (Reporting by Krista Hughes; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-10-13T23:12:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151010ebaa00l7x\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 European Markets Finished Mostly Higher Ahead Of Fed Announcement\b0\par\par\uc2 1,003 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2015 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - The majority of the European markets ended Thursday's session in the green. The mood among investors was one of caution ahead of the upcoming announcement from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . Experts remain divided on whether the Fed will decide to raise interest rates or hold off until December.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  left its key interest rates unchanged in the negative zone and vowed to remain active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market despite recent depreciation of the franc.\par \par \uc2 Due to renewed fall in oil prices, negative inflation is forecast to be deeper than the bank estimated in June.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate on sight deposit at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was maintained at -0.75 percent, and the target range for the three-month libor between -1.25 percent and -0.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 The negative interest rates in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the SNB's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as required in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market make investments in Swiss francs less attractive. These factors helped to ease the pressure on the franc, the SNB noted.\par \par \uc2 The Euro Stoxx 50 index of eurozone bluechip stocks increased by 0.12 percent, while the Stoxx Europe 50 index, which includes some major U.K. companies, lost 0.30 percent.\par \par \uc2 The DAX of Germany climbed by 0.02 percent and the CAC 40 of France rose by 0.20 percent. The FTSE of the U.K. fell by 0.68 percent and the SMI of Switzerland finished lower by 0.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Frankfurt, utilities bounced back from the sharp declines of the past 2 days. RWE surged by 9.02 percent and \uc2 E.ON\uc2  gained 8.00 percent. RWE is reportedly in talks about a joint venture with an investor in Abu Dhabi.\par \par \uc2 Shares of Adidas increased by 3.66 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Paris, Renault advanced by 1.62 percent and \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  rose by 2.03 percent.\par \par \uc2 In London, \uc2 Merlin Entertainments\uc2  dipped by 0.03 percent. The company reported that its total revenue growth for the 36 weeks ended September 5 was 2.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Glencore\uc2  decreased by 1.89 percent on reports that it is in talks to sell portions of the future production of three South American copper mines to some Canadian companies.\par \par \uc2 The rest of the mining stocks were also under pressure. Antofagasta declined by 2.48 percent and Fresnillo lost 2.72 percent. \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  weakened by 2.25 percent and \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  surrendered 1.86 percent.\par \par \uc2 Rotork plunged by 10.83 percent. The firm forecast annual revenue below analysts' expectations.\par \par \uc2 Smiths Group dropped 4.16 percent and \uc2 Weir Group\uc2  fell 2.58 percent.\par \par \uc2 Ocado\uc2  Group surged by 8.42 percent, after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  upgraded its rating on the stock to "Hold" from "Sell."\par \par \uc2 Premier Farnell\uc2  dropped by 15.98 percent, after first-half profit before tax fell. The company also announced a reduction in dividend.\par \par \uc2 Phoenix Group\uc2  rose 0.46 percent. The firm has been evaluating Guardian Financial Services as part of an on-going sale process for the business. Discussions remain on a non-exclusive basis, it added.\par \par \uc2 Altice gained 0.68 percent in Amsterdam. The company and \uc2 Cablevision Systems Corp\uc2 . have entered into a definitive agreement for Altice to acquire Cablevision in a deal with an enterprise value of $17.7 billion.\par \par \uc2 Bank stocks surged in Madrid after receiving broker upgrades. Bankia soared by 9.25 percent, after \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  upgraded its rating on the stock. \uc2 Banco de Sabadell\uc2  gained 6.30 percent after an upgrade from \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . \uc2 Banco Popular Espanol\uc2  also rose by 4.58 percent.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone construction output rebounded in July, logging the biggest growth in two years, \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  reported Thursday. Construction output grew 1 percent in July from June, when it fell 1.2 percent. This was the fastest growth since August 2014, when it rose 1.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Greece's unemployment rate decreased more-than-expected in the three months ended June, figures from the \uc2 Hellenic Statistical Authority\uc2  showed Thursday. The jobless rate fell to 24.6 percent in the second quarter from 26.6 percent in the previous quarter. Economists had forecast the rate to decrease to 25.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 U.K. retail sales grew for the first time in three months in August as school uniform sales boosted clothing turnover. Retail sales including automotive fuel advanced 0.2 percent month-on-month in August after staying flat a month ago, figures from the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  revealed Thursday. The rate matched economists' expectations.\par \par \uc2 First-time claims for U.S. unemployment benefits unexpectedly decreased in the week ended September 12th, according to a report released by the Labor Department on Thursday, with jobless claims falling to a nearly two-month low.\par \par \uc2 The report said initial jobless claims fell to 264,000, a decrease of 11,000 from the previous week's unrevised level of 275,000. Economists had expected jobless claims to come in unchanged.\par \par \uc2 New residential construction in the U.S. decreased in the month of August, according to a report released by the Commerce Department on Thursday, although the report also showed a notable increase in building permits.\par \par \uc2 The report said housing starts fell 3.0 percent to an annual rate of 1.126 million in August from the revised July estimate of 1.161. Economists had expected housing starts to drop to a rate of 1.168 million from the 1.206 million originally reported for the previous month, reflecting a 3.2 percent decrease.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing conditions in the mid-Atlantic region were mixed in September, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia\uc2 , although the headline index of regional manufacturing activity unexpectedly indicated a contraction.\par \par \uc2 A report released by the Philly Fed on Thursday showed that the diffusion index of current activity tumbled to a negative 6.0 in September from a positive 8.3 in August. The negative reading indicates a contraction in manufacturing activity.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2015 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020150917eb9h005xx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 SNB Keeps Negative Rates Unchanged; Sees Deep Deflation\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2015\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2015 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  left its key interest rates unchanged in the negative zone and vowed to remain active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market despite recent depreciation of the franc.\par \par \uc2 Due to renewed fall in oil prices, negative inflation is forecast to be deeper than the bank estimated in June.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate on sight deposit at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was maintained at -0.75 percent, and the target range for the three-month libor between -1.25 percent and -0.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 The negative interest rates in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the SNB's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as required in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market make investments in Swiss francs less attractive. These factors helped to ease the pressure on the franc, the SNB noted.\par \par \uc2 In January, the SNB unexpectedly discontinued the minimum exchange rate of CHF 1.2 per euro, resulting in a sharp appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc is still significantly overvalued, despite a slight depreciation, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 As long as the current depreciation continue, Julien Manceaux, an \uc2 ING Bank NV\uc2  economist does not see any more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the SNB. However, this depreciation could be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 In the coming months, the economist believes that the SNB will be increasingly tolerant with higher values of the Swiss franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The bank vowed to remain active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as necessary, in order to take account of the impact of the exchange rate situation on inflation and economic developments.\par \par \uc2 Owing primarily to the fall in oil prices, the forecast for the short term was somewhat lower than that presented in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Inflation was estimated to be at -1.2 percent this year instead of -1 percent estimated in June. The rate for 2016 was revised to -0.5 percent from -0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The SNB continues to expect inflation to move back into positive territory at the beginning of 2017. It is expected to average 0.4 percent in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Further, the bank said economic activity will pick up gradually in the second half of the year. For the current year, it still expects real growth of close to 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 State Secretariat for Economic Affairs\uc2  said earlier today the Swiss economy is likely to remain subdued in the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the government agency raised the growth outlook to 0.9 percent from 0.8 percent for 2015. However, the projection for 2016 was lowered to 1.5 percent from 1.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2015 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020150917eb9h0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
