\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  still ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  -Zurbruegg in paper\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2015\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Zurbruegg: franc still "strongly overvalued"\par \par \uc2 * Sees worst over for Swiss economy after first half\par \par \uc2 * 'Too big to fail' bank risk not completely eliminated\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still ready to step into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed to rein in the Swiss franc, policymaker Fritz Zurbruegg told a newspaper, calling the franc "strongly overvalued" despite its recent dip.\par \par \uc2 "We are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it will be necessary from a monetary policy perspective," Zurbruegg, vice chairman of the Swiss National Bank (SNB), told Finanz und Wirtschaft in an interview published on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "We made this clear for example after announcement of the referendum (on potential bailout terms) in Greece as there were increased safe-haven flows of funds into the franc."\par \par \uc2 The SNB gave rare confirmation in June that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken a franc whose strength has hamstrung \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 The SNB in January abandoned efforts to keep the euro above 1.20 francs, sending the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring. The euro now trades around 1.0780 francs, its strongest since February but still below the level the SNB once sought to defend.\par \par \uc2 The paper had posted most of the interview on its website on Friday, citing Zurbruegg as saying the SNB will maintain its negative interest rates despite the franc's dip.\par \par \uc2 At its last policy meeting in June, the SNB kept its target range for three-month Libor rates at -1.25 to -0.25 percent and a 0.75 percent charge on some cash deposits.\par \par \uc2 In the print version of the interview, Zurbruegg said the worst should be over for Swiss economic growth after the first half of the year. "We are counting here on recovering global economic growth that should support demand for Swiss products."\par \par \uc2 Zurbruegg laid some responsibility for the franc's surge on Swiss investors who have sharply scaled back foreign investment. "We thus have to reduce the overall attraction of the franc. That works only if the market rates fall overall," he said.\par \par \uc2 Zurbruegg dismissed suggestions that the SNB put a large part of its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves into a special fund, saying managing reserves was a central part of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 He also said the risk that major banks like \uc2 UBS\uc2  or \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  were too big to fail had not been completely eliminated, noting crisis plans needed improvement.\par \par \uc2 "More measures are needed to strengthen the Swiss package, especially in the area of equity," he said, adding big banks had improved capital ratios when measured by risk-weighted assets but had room to improve when it comes to leverage ratios.\par \par \uc2 Domestically focused banks were well capitalised but should be very cautious on lending and interest rate risk, he added.\par \par \uc2 He cited imbalances in the residential property market that made it too early to sound the all-clear on bubble risks. (Reporting by Michael Shields; Editing by Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-8T11:33:32.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150808eb88009c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
