\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Swiss rates to stay negative despite franc dip - SNB's Zurbruegg\b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2015\par 18:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes from interview)\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its negative interest rates despite a welcome dip in the Swiss franc that remains "strongly overvalued", policymaker Fritz Zurbruegg told a Swiss newspaper.\par \par \uc2 "We don't see any reason at the moment to change anything," Zurbruegg, vice chairman of the Swiss National Bank's governing board, told Finanz und Wirtschaft in an interview when asked about the SNB's 0.75 percent charge on some deposits.\par \par \uc2 He spoke as the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose for the first time in four months in July, hitting their highest under current records as the franc dipped against the euro amid likely \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro is trading around 1.0760 francs, its strongest since February but still much weaker than the 1.20 limit the SNB once sought to defend.\par \par \uc2 At its last policy meeting in June, the SNB kept its target range for three-month Libor rates at -1.25 to -0.25 percent and the charge on some cash deposits.\par \par \uc2 Zurbruegg said negative rates were starting to work. "But the franc is still strongly overvalued," he added, saying the situation remained very challenging given the franc's surge after the SNB removed a franc cap against the euro in January .\par \par \uc2 "The weakening of the franc in recent weeks is going in the right direction," he said in interview posted on the paper's website.\par \par \uc2 Asked where the bottom for rates was, he said: "That is unclear because the costs for holding cash cannot be clearly determined. Although we went further than any other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with -0.75 percent, we have not seen increased demand for cash."\par \par \uc2 He said the negative rate was having the desired impact although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was monitoring developments closely. "Of course we consider how far we can reduce the rate without having an impact on demand for cash," he added.\par \par \uc2 Zurbruegg said the SNB would keep negative rates as long as needed although "sooner or later a normalisation of rates will come." Still, low rates are in store for some time while the world economy is recovering so slowly, he added.\par \par \uc2 The SNB gave rare confirmation in June that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to weaken the franc, whose strength has hamstrung \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-reliant economy. Zurbruegg did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interview. (Reporting by Michael Shields and John Miller; Editing by Janet Lawrence/Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-7T17:46:53.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150807eb8700ldt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
