\par\par \b \uc2 SNB targets summer market lull to weaken Swiss franc\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2015\par 14:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is using the window of opportunity created by a summer lull in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to push down the value of the Swiss franc with less effort and at lower cost.\par \par \uc2 Data and price action support the view among traders that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has made several such moves in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The franc is hovering near four-month lows against the euro and hit a more than three-month low versus the dollar this week, with the options market suggesting the bias for bets in favour of further gains in the franc, especially against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is gradually waning.\par \par \uc2 Data from the SNB on Monday suggested it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, right in the middle of the traditional lull in July and August, when asset managers and traders go on holiday and volumes are at least 10-15 percent lower than the average $5 trillion a day.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves tend to be larger in a less liquid market, increasing the chances of the SNB's efforts bearing fruit.\par \par \uc2 "In thin volumes, they are the big guys," said Peter Rosenstreich, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 , Geneva. "They are trying to get the exchange rate away from the levels they are uncomfortable with, while the (SNB's balance sheet) losses are not large at the same time."\par \par \uc2 The SNB, one of the few major central banks that invests in stocks including Apple shares, posted a record loss of 50.1 billion Swiss francs ($51.8 billion) in the first half as it stepped up its purchases of euros, whose value has fallen during the 1-trillion-euro asset purchase programme the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicked off in March.\par \par \uc2 The huge loss prompted the SNB to warn its shareholders, which include the federal government and regional cantons, that it may mot be able to maintain its regular dividend payout policy, drawing criticism within the country.\par \par \uc2 Given the losses and the fact that the SNB's balance sheet has ballooned to more than 85 percent of gross domestic product, analysts do not expect the SNB to be very aggressive with its current phase of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That does not rule out some modest and passive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but we see this as a holding exercise until broad dollar strength takes dollar/Swiss franc back above parity - and helps to soften the trade-weighted franc," said Chris Turner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at ING.\par \par \uc2 EURO BUYER\par \par \uc2 Having stunned global markets in January by abandoning the franc's 1.20 per euro cap it had defended since 2011, the SNB has since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in fits and starts.\par \par \uc2 Its presence prevented the franc from hitting parity against the euro during the height of the Greek debt crisis. The pressure was so acute that the SNB issued an unusual public confirmation that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  data suggests the SNB may have been the only major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying euros in the first quarter of the year, a time when others were cutting their exposure.\par \par \uc2 Of late, though, with the Greek crisis slightly less acute and deflation risk increasing in Switzerland, investors have been cutting exposure to the franc.\par \par \uc2 Elsa Lignos, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC Capital, recommended investors to bet on long-term euro appreciation against the Swiss franc via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options.\par \par \uc2 "Part of its reasoning for abandoning the floor was to allow room for further balance sheet expansion in extreme circumstances," Lignos said. "We think the time is right to reposition in the euro/Swiss franc pair." (Reporting by Anirban Nag, editing by John Stonestreet) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-5T13:05:59.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150805eb8500i56\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
