\par\par \b \uc2 Commodity-Linked  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Soft; USD Buoyant on Yellen, U.S. Data -- ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK\b0\par\par\uc2 2,029 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2015\par 02:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par         Immediate Range Larger Range \par USD/JPY 123.23-123.99   122.91-124.46 \par EUR/USD 1.0928-1.1034   1.0915-1.1081 \par AUD/USD 0.7351-0.7488   0.7240-0.7501 \par NZD/USD 0.6559-0.6723   0.6192-0.6752 \par GBP/USD 1.5575-1.5674   1.5449-1.5732 \par USD/CHF 0.9436-0.9550   0.9399-0.9598 \par USD/CAD 1.2885-1.2958   1.2715-1.3000 \par EUR/JPY 135.25-136.37   133.72-137.76 \par EUR/GBP 0.6981-0.7068   0.6981-0.7137 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting three-week high 123.97 Wednesday. Underpinned by positive dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 97.13 versus 96.60 early Wednesday) after Federal Reserve Chairwoman Yellen reaffirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained on a path to raise interest rates this year in her semi-annual testimony before the \uc2 House Financial Services Committee\uc2 ; higher-than-expected 0.4% on-month rise in U.S. June PPI (versus forecast +0.2%); healthier-than-expected U.S. July Empire State manufacturing index of 3.9 (versus forecast 3.0); stronger-than-expected 0.3% on-month increase in U.S. June industrial production (forecast +0.2%) & capacity utilization of 78.4% (versus forecast 78.1%). USD/JPY also supported by demand from Japan importers; ultra-loose \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  monetary policy. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales; lower \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields (10-year slipped 4.3 bps to 2.355% Wednesday). Data focus: 0500 GMT Japan July \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  monthly report; 1230 GMT U.S. jobless claims in week ended July 11; 1300 GMT U.S. May \uc2 Treasury\uc2  international capital data; 1400 GMT U.S. July NAHB housing market index; 1400 GMT U.S. July \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  business outlook survey; 1830 GMT Yellen presents monetary policy report to U.S. Senate Banking Committee. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, five-day moving average above 15-day moving average and advancing. Resistance at 123.97-123.99 (Wednesday's high-June 26 high); breach would target 124.38-124.46 band (June 24 high-June 17 high), then 124.63 (June 10 high), 124.74 (June 9 high) and 125.68 (June 8 high). Support at 123.23 (Wednesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 122.91 (Tuesday's low), then 122.00 (Monday's low), 121.55 (Friday's low), 120.44-120.38 band (July 9 low-July 8 low), psychological 120.00 line and 119.22 (May 18 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD--to trade in lower range. Undermined by uncertainty over Greece: the Greek parliament approved the EUR86 billion bailout agreement proposed by creditors--by 229 votes in Greece's 300-seat Parliament--but the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said Greece still needs debt relief, while Greek protests broke out against harsh terms on the fresh bailout plan. EUR/USD also weighed by positive dollar sentiment; \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large-scale quantitative easing program; euro sales on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0900 GMT eurozone May trade balance; 0900 GMT eurozone June final harmonized CPI; 1145 GMT \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy decision. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish, five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support at 1.0928 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 1.0915 (July 7 low), then 1.0887 (June 1 low) and 1.0819 (May 27 reaction low). Resistance at 1.1034 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 1.1081 (Tuesday's high), then 1.1196 (Monday's high), 1.1215 (Friday's high), 1.1243 (June 30 high), 1.1278 (June 29 high) and 1.1347 (June 23 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting a six-year low 0.7351 Wednesday. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment; soft commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 1.26% at 216.62 Wednesday). But AUD/USD losses tempered by Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. Data focus: 0100 GMT Australia July consumer inflationary expectations survey; 0130 GMT Australia June official reserve asset. Daily chart negative-biased as bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Wednesday; MACD bearish; stochastics stays suppressed at oversold levels; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support at 0.7351 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7240 (May 1, 2009 low), then the psychological 0.7000 line. Resistance at 0.7488 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7496-0.7501 (Friday's high-July 7 high), then 0.7533 (July 6 high), 0.7648-0.7656 band (July 3 high-July 2 high), 0.7738 (July 1 high), 0.7752 (June 25 high) and 0.7771 (June 24 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting five-year low 0.6561 this morning. NZD sentiment hurt by 10.7% drop in Fonterra's GDT Price Index and 13.1% drop in average price for whole milk powder to $1,848/ton at latest GlobalDairyTrade auction. NZD/USD also weighed by positive dollar sentiment; divergent Reserve Bank of New Zealand-Federal Reserve monetary policy stances; Kiwi sales on buoyant AUD/NZD cross; softer-than-expected New Zealand 2Q CPI of +0.4% on-quarter (versus forecast +0.6%). Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics bearish; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support at 0.6559 (May 25, 2010 reaction low); breach would expose downside to 0.6192 (July 13, 2009 low), then 0.6148 (June 8, 2009 low) and the psychological 0.6000. Resistance at 0.6723 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.6742-0.6752 band (Tuesday's high-Monday's high), then 0.6771 (Friday's high), 0.6810 (July 1 high), 0.6854 (June 30 high), 0.6880 (June 29 high) and 0.6924 (June 25 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD--to consolidate after hitting two-week high 1.5674 Wednesday. Sterling sentiment dented by higher-than-expected U.K. unemployment rate of 5.6% in the three months to May (versus forecast 5.5%), weaker-than-expected 2.8% on-year rise in average weekly earnings excluding bonuses in the 3 months to May (versus forecast +3.0%), surprise 7,000 increase in U.K. June jobless claimants (versus forecast -10,000). GBP/USD also weighed by positive dollar sentiment. But GBP/USD losses tempered by sterling demand on soft EUR/GBP cross; hawkish comments on U.K. interest rates from Bank of England Governor Carney and BOE's David Miles on Tuesday. Data & event focus: 0830 GMT U.K. June \uc2 CML\uc2  mortgage lending; 1800 GMT Bank of England Governor \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  speech. Daily chart tilting positive as stochastics rising from oversold levels; negative MACD histogram bars contracting. Resistance at 1.5674 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5732 (July 1 high), then 1.5787 (June 29 high) and 1.5802 (June 24 high). Support at 1.5575 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5449 (Tuesday's low), then 1.5361 (Friday's low), 1.5341 (July 9 low), 1.5328 (July 8 low), 1.5276 (100-day moving average), 1.5256 (June 9 low) and 1.5219 (June 8 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting two-and-a-half month high 0.9550 Wednesday. Underpinned by positive dollar sentiment; drop in Switzerland ZEW - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  indicator of economic sentiment to -5.4 in July from 0.1 in June; threat of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; negative Swiss interest rates. Data focus: 0715 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  May retail sales. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought levels. Resistance at 0.9550 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 0.9598 (April 28 high), then 0.9718 (April 23 reaction high). Support at 0.9436 (Wednesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 0.9399 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9365 (Monday's low), 0.9329 (Friday's low), 0.9241 (June 29 low), 0.9207 (June 23 low), 0.9152-0.9145 band (June 22 low-June 18 low) and 0.9108 (May 15 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting six-year high 1.2958 Wednesday. Loonie sentiment hurt after \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  cut its key interest rate to 0.5% from 0.75%. USD/CAD also supported by positive dollar sentiment; weaker-than-expected 0.1% on-month increase in Canada May manufacturing sales (versus forecast +0.4%); softer oil prices (\uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude settled down $1.63 at $51.41/bbl Wednesday). Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada May international transactions in securities; 1230 GMT Canada 2Q investment in non-residential building construction. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought levels; five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 1.2958 (Wednesday's high); breach would target psychological 1.3000 line, then 1.3063 (March 9, 2009 swing high) and 1.3166 (Sept. 1, 2004 high). Support at 1.2885 (hourly chart), then at 1.2719-1.2715 (Wednesday's low-Tuesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 1.2668 (Monday's low), then 1.2652 (July 9 low), 1.2640 (July 7 low), 1.2560 (July 6 low), 1.2534 (July 3 low) and 1.2471 (July 1 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY--to