\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-What can the Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  do to stem the franc's rise?\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2015\par 14:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, July 3 (Reuters) - Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fighting to keep the value of the franc down as Greece enters a new period of uncertainty and political turmoil that may spur safe-haven inflows into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has given rare confirmation that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the franc's rise, which is the main focus of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Since dropping its limit on the franc's value at 1.20 per euro in January, the SNB has imposed negative interest rates, a charge on some cash deposits and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-reliant economy already hit hard by the franc's strength and negative inflation forecast this year and next, the SNB is seen left with few policy options.\par \par \uc2 Here are main scenarios that economists say face the SNB:\par \par \uc2 ASSET PURCHASES\par \par \uc2 Switzerland could consider easing monetary policy further by launching a programme to buy foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets and some domestic assets, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  recommended in March.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably doesn't want to tie itself to a precise amount of asset purchases, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Maxime Botteron said, making a set programme unlikely.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Pledges to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, along with negative interest rates, are the SNB's main policy tool at the moment. Buying euros and selling francs would help hold down the franc, according to economists, but may bulk up the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet. The SNB cited balance sheet risk as a key reason for dropping its long-standing cap in January. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, at 517.5 billion francs ($550.1 billion) in May, have swollen as a result of the SNB's moves.\par \par \uc2 BOND BUYING\par \par \uc2 The ECB\uc2  has launched a 1 trillion-euro "quantitative easing" scheme to buy government bonds and other assets and stimulate a sagging euro zone economy. The SNB tried buying back government bonds following the financial crisis, but the measure was not seen as successful in easing monetary conditions. Economists say Switzerland's small and illiquid government bond market, where most investors hold bonds to maturity, is not suited for a large-scale bond repurchase programme.\par \par \uc2 LOWER INTEREST RATES\par \par \uc2 The SNB said it hasn't reached the lower bound for the Swiss franc cash deposit rate, now at -0.75 percent, and could push it further into negative territory. Economists say it would probably lower benchmark interest rates at the same time.\par \par \uc2 This could trigger cash hoarding to avoid the deposit charges. It could also fuel a housing bubble, which the SNB has fought by tightening capital requirements for mortgage lenders.\par \par \uc2 NEW FRANC CAP\par \par \uc2 Swiss unions and left-wing Social Democrat politicians have called for a new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cap to shield the economy. With the government staunchly behind the SNB and other political parties also broadly supportive of monetary policy, the proposal for a new cap is unlikely to win SNB favour.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's mettle in defending a potential new cap would also quickly be tested by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors, economists say, because the SNB gave up the original 1.20 level under pressure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  BASKET\par \par \uc2 Days before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped the cap on the euro, former SNB adviser Ernst Baltensperger suggested replacing it with one linked to a basket of euro and U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The SNB was lukewarm when asked at June's policy meeting about linking the franc to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it doesn't fundamentally solve the franc problem.\par \par \uc2 CAPITAL CONTROLS\par \par \uc2 While highly unlikely and requiring government backing, Switzerland could impose controls on cash withdrawals from banks.\par \par \uc2 The SNB has signalled it would not resort to such a drastic measure but says it has not excluded any potential policy tools, including capital controls.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.9407 Swiss francs) (Reporting by Katharina Bart)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-7-6T12:25:24.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150703eb7300d49\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
