\par\par \uc2 Economics Blog\par \b \uc2 Grand Central: A Tale of Two Cities: Basel and Athens\b0\par\par\uc2 2,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2015\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Energy Stories\par \uc2 DJTES\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's Daily Report on Global Central Banks for Monday, June 29, 2015:\par \par \uc2 Sign up for the newsletter:\u160\'00\'A0  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup }}}\par \par \uc2 Highlights\par \par \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 's Take: A Tale of Two Cities: Basel and Athens\par \par \uc2 Greece Orders Banks Closed, Imposes Capital Controls to Stem Deposit Flight\par \par \uc2 China Struggles to Balance Volatile Markets\par \par \uc2 BIS Warns of Overreliance on Monetary Policy\par \par \uc2 Fed's George: Payments System Needs Improvement\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTONE\uc2 'S TAKE: A TALE OF TWO CITIES: BASEL AND ATHENS\par \par \uc2 This weekend brought a stark contrast in the world of central banking: how the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  thinks {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/bis-warns-of-overreliance-on-monetary-policy-1435487425"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it should be }}}\uc2 , and how the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and others see it amidst the fallout from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/SB11064341213388534269604581075703841095260"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the meltdown in Greece }}}\uc2  \u160\'00\'A0 that now includes bank closures and capital controls.\par \par \uc2 Here's the weekend recap.\par \par \uc2 In its annual report published Sunday the BIS--a Basel, Switzerland-based consortium of central banks--repeated its warnings of recent years that ultra-loose monetary policies may do more harm than good.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy has been overburdened for far too long," the BIS said\uc2 . "It must be part of the answer but cannot be the whole answer."\par \par \uc2 Its reasoning is that low interest rates may lead to more financial booms and busts. These weigh on the global economy's growth potential, meaning ongoing low interest rates, rising debt and a continuation of a negative cycle.\par \par \uc2 "In short, low rates beget lower rates," the BIS said.\par \par \uc2 And this line of reasoning makes sense. Central banks, particularly in the U.S. and U.K. where economies have recovered, still have crisis-like interest rates near zero. And other generally healthy economies such as Denmark, Sweden and Switzerland have negative policy rates to shield their economies from any fallout from \uc2 the ECB\uc2 's quantitative easing program.\par \par \uc2 At some point, some major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must raise rates or put their economies at risk of destabilizing asset bubbles in housing and other rate-sensitive sectors.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 the ECB\uc2  is a cautionary tale. Of the world's major central banks, it is already near the end of the line when it comes to the prospects for tightening policy. Its quantitative easing program isn't slated to end until September 2016 and could stretch further.\par \par \uc2 But faced with a Greek debt default, \uc2 the ECB\uc2  has already signaled it can do more to protect the eurozone. "The Governing Council is determined to use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2015/html/pr150628.en.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all the instruments }}}\uc2  available within its mandate," it said Sunday, after declining to add to an emergency lending lifeline for Greek banks beyond the roughly 89 billion euros that is currently extended.\par \par \uc2 And it isn't just \uc2 the ECB\uc2  that is affected by Greece. If there's a messy exit from the eurozone that destabilizes global financial markets, that could make it harder for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise rates as soon as September (after all, \uc2 the ECB\uc2  hiked rates around two months before the Lehman collapse in 2008, and that didn't turn out so well).\par \par \uc2 Sweden, Denmark and Switzerland\u8212\'20\'14which are outside the eurozone but dependent on what happens there\u8212\'20\'14may be forced to ease more to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much.\par \par \uc2 So on the one hand, the BIS is right: the era of super-accommodative monetary policy can't continue indefinitely. But events in Athens show how hard it is for central bankers to follow this advice.\par \par \uc2 -By Brian Blackstone\par \par \uc2 MORNING MINUTES: KEY DEVELOPMENTS AROUND THE WORLD\par \par \uc2 Greece Orders Banks Closed, Imposes Capital Controls to Stem Deposit Flight. \u160\'00\'A0 Greece shut down its banking system, ordering lenders to stay closed for six days starting Monday, and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved to impose controls to prevent money from flooding out of the country.\u160\'00\'A0 The steps, a fateful climax to five years of debt crisis, put Greece closer than it ever has been to an exit from the euro and pushes the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/SB11064341213388534269604581075703841095260"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 into uncharted waters }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Stabilize Franc Amid Greek Concerns. \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Sunday night to stabilize the strong franc, which has faced renewed buying triggered by the concerns over  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/swiss-central-bank-intervened-to-stabilize-franc-amid-greek-concerns-1435572993"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a potential Greek exit from the euro }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What Yellen And Other U.S. Officials  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/28/what-yellen-and-other-u-s-officials-have-said-about-greece-and-the-global-economy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Have Said About Greece }}}\uc2  and the Global Economy.\par \par \uc2 China Struggles to Balance Volatile Markets. \u160\'00\'A0 As the past trading week drew to a close, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  was getting increasingly nervous about the money evaporating from Chinese stocks. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided it was time for an about-face,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/pboc-struggles-to-balance-volatile-markets-1435520232"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quickly moving to ease credit }}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/briefly/2015/06/28/5-questions-about-the-pbocs-latest-easing-measures/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 5 Questions }}}\uc2  About the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's Latest Easing Measures.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/28/economists-react-chinas-central-bank-moves-to-buoy-stock-market-confidence/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Economists React }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BIS Warns of Overreliance on Monetary Policy. \u160\'00\'A0 The \uc2 Bank for International Settlements{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/bis-warns-of-overreliance-on-monetary-policy-1435487425"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a stark warning }}}\uc2  Sunday that the feeble global economy remains too dependent on monetary policy to stimulate demand in the absence of more lasting structural reforms.\par \par \uc2 BIS: Fed Should Work Closely With Other Central Banks.\u160\'00\'A0 The BIS\u160\'00\'A0 said \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials\u160\'00\'A0  should cooperate more closely with other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy makers, and possibly set rules that would\u160\'00\'A0  limit their individual freedom of action to\u160\'00\'A0  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/SB11363635366385803997104581072131926370472"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 avoid excessive tightening or loosening }}}\uc2 \u160\'00\'A0 of monetary policy at a global level.\par \par \uc2 BIS' Graphic Error Leaves Greece Out of Eurozone. The eurozone has already lost Greece. At least, that's what a map included in the BIS's annual report appears to show. It is, of course,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/for-bis-greeces-omission-is-a-graphic-error-1435352329"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an embarrassing error }}}\uc2  rather than a product of the BIS' insight into the fate of the eurozone's most troubled member. The graphic also omits Malta, Slovenia, Slovakia, Luxembourg and Cyprus from the euro's reach even though all are members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area.\par \par \uc2 Fed's George: Payments System Needs Improvement. In a speech highlighting the need for\u160\'00\'A0  improving the U.S. payments system,\u160\'00\'A0 Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City President \uc2 Esther George\uc2  said there's  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/26/feds-esther-george-u-s-payments-system-needs-improvement/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a renewed focus on bolstering security }}}\uc2 . Among industry participants and central bankers, "I sense a greater degree of consensus around the security challenges we face," Ms. George said Friday.\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 The Fed\uc2  plays a significant role in providing the infrastructure to move money around in the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Fed's Dudley Reiterates Rate Hike Possible by Year's End.\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  policymakers could be inclined to hike interest rates as soon as September, or may equally feel a delay until December is justified should economic data soften, a key central banker has said. \u160\'00\'A0 "It would not shock me if we decided to lift off in September," \uc2 William Dudley\uc2 , president of the \uc2 New York Federal Reserve Bank\uc2 , told the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  in an interview Friday and posted on the newspaper's website Monday. But, added Mr. Dudley, "it wouldn't shock me if the data were a little softer and it caused us to wait" until December--Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Quieter April for Yellen. After a flurry of Capitol Hill meetings in March, Fed Chairwoman\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 \u160\'00\'A0 met with just two lawmakers in April, both Democrats. Ms. Yellen met with Sen.\u160\'00\'A0 Sherrod Brown\u160\'00\'A0 (D., Ohio) and separately with Sen.\u160\'00\'A0 Patty Murray\u160\'00\'A0 (D., Wash.),  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/26/a-quieter-april-for-feds-janet-yellen-after-a-flurry-of-march-meetings/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to her monthly calendar }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC CONTENT\par \par \uc2 U.S. Stands Out. The U.S. has become an outlier among its peers in a key employment gauge. Among eight major advanced economies, all but one \u8212\'20\'14 the United States \u8212\'20\'14 show gains in labor force participation over the past 15 years, according to a\u160\'00\'A0  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2015/june/labor-force-participation-the-us-and-its-peers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new study }}}\uc2 \u160\'00\'A0 by \uc2 Maximiliano Dvorkin\uc2 \u160\'00\'A0 and\u160\'00\'A0 Hannah Shell\u160\'00\'A0 of the\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis\uc2 . Participation fell by 4.6 percentage points in the U.S. between 1997 and 2013, says the study based on data from the\u160\'00\'A0 \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2 . By contrast, it rose over that period in Canada, France, Germany, Japan, Spain, Sweden  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/26/the-u-s-stands-out-on-labor-force-participation-rates/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and the United Kingdom }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORWARD GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 MONDAY\par \par \uc2 -1400 GMT (10 a.m. EDT): BOE's Haldane remarks, to be delivered Tuesday, released\par \par \uc2 TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 -0935 GMT (5:35 a.m. EDT): \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 's Dudley speaks in Basel, Switzerland\par \par \uc2 -1300 GMT (9 a.m. EDT): ECB's Nowotny speaks in Vienna\par \par \uc2 -6 p.m. EDT: \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2 's Bullard speaks in St. Louis, Mo.\par \par \uc2 WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 -ECB holds a non-monetary policy meeting in Frankfurt\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 National Bank of Georgia\uc2  releases a policy statement\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 National Bank of Romania\uc2  releases a policy statement\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Bank of Albania\uc2  releases a policy statement\par \par \uc2 THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 -0730 GMT (3:30 a.m. EDT): \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  releases a policy statement\par \par \uc2 -1130 GMT (7:30 a.m. EDT): ECB releases minutes of its June 3 policy meeting\par \par \uc2 -8:30 a.m. EDT: U.S. Labor Department releases June employment report\par \par \uc2 -1510 GMT (11:10 a.m. EDT): ECB's Draghi speaks in Milan, Italy\par \par \uc2 -1630 GMT (12:30 p.m. EDT): ECB's Mersch speaks in Milan, Italy\par \par \uc2 FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 -U.S. markets closed for Independence Day holiday\par \par \uc2 -0610 GMT (2:10 a.m. EDT): ECB's Constancio speaks in Vilnius, Lithuania\par \par \uc2 -0800 GMT (4 a.m. EDT): ECB's Nowotny speaks in Vienna\par \par \uc2 RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 Get Used to Part-Time Work.  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://macroblog.typepad.com/macroblog/2015/06/will-the-elevated-share-of-part-time-workers-last.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Atlanta Fed's }}}\uc2  Ellie Terry uses surveys from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to show that the high share of part-time employment in the U.S. is likely to persist. "Higher compensation costs of full-time relative to part-time employees and the role of technology that enables companies to more easily manage their workforce can be considered structural factors influencing the behavior of firms," she writes.\par \par \uc2 IMF\uc2 : Fed Should Wait Until Mid-2016 to Raise Rates. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  should wait until the middle of 2016 to start raising short-term interest rates from near zero to ensure the economy is on a firmer footing and inflation is returning to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target after undershooting it for more than three years. That's the message from a new working paper from  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2015/06/29/fed-should-keep-rates-at-zero-until-mid-2016-to-avoid-dark-corners-imf-paper-says/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the International Monetary Fund }}}\uc2  that lends some technical chops to the argument the fund's Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  has been making to both U.S. and European officials: The world economy is still fragile and inflation very low, so don't rush to tighten monetary policy just yet.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY\par \par \uc2 Shelter from the Storm in Europe. On Project Syndicate,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.project-syndicate.org/commentary/greece-ukraine-populist-parties-europe-crises-by-mohamed-a--el-erian-2015-06"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian }}}\uc2  looks beyond the Greek crisis to other looming European crises involving the war in Ukraine and the rise of anti-immigrant, anti-European and anti-austerity parties. "With three storms looming, Europe's leaders must act fast to ensure that they can dissipate each before it merges with the others, and cope effectively with whatever disruptions they cause. The good news is that regional crisis-management tools have lately been strengthened considerably, especially since the summer of 2012, when the euro came very close to collapsing," he writes.\par \par \uc2 ECB Errors Paved Way for Greek Exit. Writing on VoxEU,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.voxeu.org/article/grexit-staggering-cost-central-bank-dependence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Charles Wyplosz }}}\uc2  argues the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision not to top up its emergency liquidity assistance to Greek banks will lead to the nation's departure from the eurozone. "\uc2 The ECB\uc2 's decision is the last of a long string of ECB mistakes in this crisis," he writes. "Beyond triggering Greece's eurozone exit \u8211\'20\'13 thus revoking the euro's irrevocability \u8211\'20\'13 it has shattered eurozone governance and brought the politicisation of \uc2 the ECB\uc2  to new heights. Bound to follow are chaos in Greece and agitation of financial markets \u8211\'20\'13 both with unknown consequences."\par \par \uc2 BASIS POINTS\par \par \uc2 Consumers Upbeat. The \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  final June sentiment index came in at 96.1,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/articles/consumers-end-june-on-a-happy-note-1435328105"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 well above }}}\uc2  the end-of-May level of 90.7.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone manufacturers lost some of their optimism in June as order books thinned, but a monthly survey carried out by the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  found little sign of alarm among businesses about the threat of a Greek exit from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  area.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone Inflation Likely Slowed in June: WSJ Economists Surveyed. According to median forecasts by economists polled by The Wall Street Journal, consumer prices likely rose 0.2% in the year ended in June. This would be a slower rate than May's 0.3% annual increase and still much beneath the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target of below, but close to, 2%. \u8211\'20\'13 Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 SIGN UP: Grand Central,  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 straight to your inbox }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FEEDBACK LOOP: Send us your tips, suggestions and feedback. Write to: Jon.Hilsenrath@wsj.com; Victoria.McGrane@wsj.com; Pedro.daCosta@wsj.com; Michael.Derby@wsj.com; Nell.Henderson@wsj.com; Brian.Blackstone@wsj.com; jason.douglas@wsj.com; Ben.Leubsdorf@wsj.com; Paul.Hannon@wsj.com; Jacob.Schlesinger@wsj.com; Sarah.Portlock@wsj.com; Kate.Davidson@wsj.com;\u160\'00\'A0 David.Harrison@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Follow us on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/wsjcentralbanks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @WSJCentralBanks }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/nhendersonwsj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NHendersonWSJ }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/pdacosta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @pdacosta }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/blackstonebrian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @Blackstonebrian }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/PaulHannon29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @PaulHannon29 }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/michaelsderby"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @michaelsderby }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/vgmac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @vgmac }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/wsj_douglasj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @wsj_douglasj }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/BenLeubsdorf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @BenLeubsdorf }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/JMSchles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JMSchles }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/MargitFeher"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @MargitFeher }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/ToddBuell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ToddBuell }}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.twitter.com/sarahportlock"}}{\fldr