ar slipped 1.6 bps to 0.593% Wednesday); Japan exporter sales; diminished investor risk appetite (VIX fear gauge rose 0.23% to 12.88, \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  closed 0.09% lower at 2,125.85 overnight after hitting record high 2,134.72). Other data: 0135 GMT Markit flash Japan May manufacturing PMI; 0430 GMT Japan March all industry index; 1230 GMT U.S. jobless claims in week ended May 16 (forecast 271,000); 1230 GMT U.S. April \uc2 Chicago Fed\uc2  National Activity Index; 1345 GMT Markit flash U.S. May manufacturing PMI (forecast 54.6 versus April's 54.1); 1400 GMT U.S. April existing home sales (forecast +1.0%), 1400 GMT U.S. April \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  leading indicators, 1400 GMT U.S. May \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  business outlook survey (forecast 8.3); 1500 GMT U.S. May \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2  manufacturing activity index (forecast -5). Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought levels; five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 121.49-121.53 (Wednesday's high-March 17 high); breach would target 121.67 (March 12 high), then 122.04 (7.5-year high hit March 10) and 123.66 (July 9, 2007 high). Support at 120.58 (Wednesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, exposing downside to 119.83 (Tuesday's low), then 119.27 (Monday's low), 119.16 (Friday's low), 118.88 (May 14 low) and 118.50 (April 30 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting three-week low 1.1062 Wednesday. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment; \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large-scale quantitative easing program; softer-than-expected Germany April PPI of +0.1% on-month, -1.5% on-year (versus forecast +0.2% on-month, -1.4% on-year); fears about Greek debt default--Greece's parliamentary speaker warned Wednesday that the country would be unable to make a EUR300 million repayment to the \uc2 IMF\uc2  on June 5 without further assistance; diminished investor risk appetite; euro sales on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0730 GMT Markit flash Germany May PMI (manufacturing PMI forecast at 52.3); 0800 GMT Markit flash eurozone May PMI (manufacturing PMI forecast at 52.1; service PMI forecast at 53.9; composite PMI forecast at 53.8); 0800 GMT eurozone March balance of payments; 1400 GMT eurozone May flash consumer confidence indicator (forecast -4.8 versus April's -4.6). Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish; five-day moving average falling below 15-day moving average. Support at 1.1062 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0959 (April 29 low), then 1.0860 (April 28 low). Resistance at 1.1153 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 1.1326 (Tuesday's high), then 1.1450 (Monday's high), 1.1468 (Friday's high), 1.1532 (Feb. 3 reaction high) and 1.1674 (Jan. 21 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting two-week low 0.7857 Wednesday. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment; soft iron ore prices (benchmark 62% grade iron fell $0.60 Wednesday to $57.80/ton); subdued investor risk appetite; dovish \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  monetary policy stance. Aussie vulnerable to 0145 GMT \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash China May manufacturing PMI data (forecast 49.3). Other data: 0100 GMT Australia May consumer inflationary expectations survey; 0130 GMT Australia April official reserve assets. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish; five-day moving average falling below 15-day moving average. Support at 0.7857 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 0.7843 (100-day moving average), then 0.7795-0.7790 (May 5 low-April 27 low, near 55-day moving average), 0.7762 (April 24 low) and 0.7680 (April 21 reaction low). Resistance at 0.7934 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 0.8010 (Tuesday's high), then 0.8051 (Monday's high), 0.8089 (Friday's high), 0.8162 (May 14 high) and 0.8233-0.8243 band (Jan. 21 high-Jan. 19 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting two-month low 0.7277 Wednesday. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment; soft dairy prices; reduced investor risk appetite; speculation that the RBNZ would cut interest rate cuts in coming months. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD interest differential. Kiwi vulnerable to 0145 GMT \uc2 HSBC\uc2  flash China May manufacturing PMI data. Other data: New Zealand Budget announcement; 0300 GMT New Zealand April credit card balances and spending. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold levels; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support 0.7277-0.7273 (Wednesday's low-March 18 reaction low); breach would expose downside to 0.7182-0.7174 band (March 11 low-Feb. 3 low), then 0.7113 (March 17, 2011 reaction low), the psychological 0.7000 line and 0.6944 (Aug. 25, 2010 low). Resistance at 0.7371 (Wednesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 0.7443 (Tuesday's high), then 0.7458 (Monday's high), 0.7502 (Friday's high), 0.7562 (May 14 high) and 0.7577 (May 5 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD--to range-trade. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment, diminished investor risk appetite. But GBP/USD downside limited by sterling demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0830 GMT U.K. April retail sales (forecast +0.4% on-month, +3.7% on-year), 0830 GMT U.K. April \uc2 CML\uc2  mortgage lending; 1000 GMT U.K. May CBI monthly industrial trends survey. Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics bearish, MACD histogram bars turning negative; although inside-day-range pattern completed Wednesday. Support at 1.5470 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 1.5445 (Tuesday's low), then 1.5391 (May 11 low), 1.5242 (May 8 low), 1.5162 (May 7 low) and 1.5149 (May 6 low). Resistance at 1.5588 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5669 (Tuesday's high), then 1.5745 (Monday's high), 1.5807-1.5814 band (Friday's high-May 14 high), 1.5825 (Nov. 27 reaction high) and 1.5944 (Nov. 11 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF--to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting two-week high 0.9406 Wednesday. Underpinned by positive dollar sentiment; negative Swiss interest rates; threat of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Swissie sentiment boosted by rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  ZEW - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  indicator of economic sentiment to minus 0.1 in May from minus 23.2 in April. USD/CHF upside also limited by franc demand on soft EUR/CHF cross. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish; five-day moving average rising above 15-day moving average. Resistance at 0.9406-0.9413 band (Wednesday's high-May 5 high); breach would target 0.9447 (April 30 high), then 0.9504 (100-day moving average), 0.9517 (200-day moving average) and 0.9574 (April 29 high). Support at 0.9336 (Wednesday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, exposing downside to 0.9240 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9140 (Monday's low), 0.9108 (Friday's low) and 0.9073-0.9065 band (May 14 low-May 7 low, near 38.2% Fibonacci correction of advance from Jan. 15 low of 0.7360 to March 12 high of 1.0128) and 0.8762 (Jan. 26 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD--to consolidate after hitting near-one-month high 1.2255 Wednesday. Underpinned by positive dollar sentiment; diminished investor risk tolerance. But USD/CAD upside limited by firmer oil prices (\uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude settled up 99 cents at $58.98 Wednesday). Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada March employment insurance. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD and stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought levels; five-day moving average above 15-day moving average and advancing. Resistance at 1.2255 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 1.2268 (April 23 high), then 1.2305 (April 21 reaction high), 1.2348 (100-day moving avera